 is hundred millions, ten millions, millions, hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, ones..\n\nWait, but when adding, we start from the right. Let me reverse them to process digit by digit correctly:.\n\nFirst number (908,835,416) digits from right to left (ones to hundred millions):.\n\n6 (ones).\n1 (tens).\n4 (hundreds).\n5 (thousands).\n3 (ten thousands).\n8 (hundred thousands).\n8 (millions).\n0 (ten millions).\n9 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number (580,931,369) digits from right to left:.\n\n9 (ones).\n6 (tens).\n3 (hundreds).\n1 (thousands).\n3 (ten thousands).\n9 (hundred thousands).\n0 (millions).\n8 (ten millions).\n5 (hundred millions).\n\nNow, adding each corresponding digit starting from ones:.\n\n1. Ones place: 6 + 9 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1..\n2. Tens place: 1 (carry) + 1 + 6 = 8. Write 8, no carry..\n3. Hundreds place: 4 + 3 = 7. Write 7..\n4. Thousands place: 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6..\n5. Ten thousands place: 3 + 3 = 6. Write 6..\n6. Hundred thousands place: 8 + 9 = 17. Write 7, carryover 1..\n7. Millions place: 8 + 0 = 8, plus carryover 1 = 9. Write 9, no carry..\n8. Ten millions place: 0 + 8 = 8. Write 8..\n9. Hundred millions place: 9 + 5 = 14. Write 14 here since there are no higher places..\n\nWait, but when writing the final sum, the digits would be from highest to lowest, so combining all the digits we wrote, starting from hundred millions down to ones. So the sum would be:.\n\nFrom hundred millions: 14 (but we can't have a two-digit number here, so this is actually 1 followed by 4 in the hundred millions and a carryover to the next higher place, but since there's no higher place, this would just be written as 14 followed by the rest). Wait, but that's not possible. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here..\n\nWait, the hundred millions digits: first number is 9, second is 5. 9 +5 =14. So when adding those, we have 14. But since that's the highest place, we just write 14. So the final sum would start with 14, then the rest of the digits. Let's put all the digits together in order:.\n\nDigits from hundred millions to ones:.\n\n14 (from 9+5), 8 (ten millions: 0+8), 9 (millions:8+0+1 carry), 7 (hundred thousands:8+9=17, write 7, carry 1), 6 (ten thousands:3+3=6), 6 (thousands:5+1=6),7 (hundreds:4+3=7),8 (tens:1+6+1=8),5 (ones:6+9=15). .\n\nWait, so putting it all together: 14 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands),7 (hundreds),8 (tens),5 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]  2%|2         | 12/500 [01:24<1:06:52,  8.22s/it]  3%|2         | 13/500 [01:27<52:44,  6.50s/it]  
