messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 833931691 and 579314615?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add these two big numbers: 833,931,691 and 579,314,615. Hmm, adding them digit by digit from right to left, just like they taught in school. Let me write them down to visualize better..\n\nFirst, align the numbers vertically:.\n\n  833,931,691.\n+579,314,615.\n-------------.\n\nStarting from the ones place. The rightmost digits are 1 and 5. 1 + 5 equals 6. So the ones digit of the sum is 6. No carry-over there..\n\nNext, the tens place: 9 + 1. That's 10. Oh, wait, so the tens digit is 0, and I carry over 1 to the hundreds place..\n\nHundreds place: 6 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) plus the carried 1. So 6 +1 is 7, plus 1 makes 8. No carry-over here..\n\nThousands place: 1 (from first number) + 4 (from second number). 1 +4 is 5. Adding any carry? No, so it's 5..\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 (from first) +1 (from second). 3+1=4. So 4 here..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 +3. 9+3=12. So write down 2 and carry over 1..\n\nMillions place: 3 (from first) +9 (from second) plus the carry 1. 3+9=12, plus 1 is 13. So 3 here, carry over 1..\n\nTen-millions place: 3 (from first) +7 (from second) plus carry 1. 3+7=10, plus 1 is 11. So 1 here, carry over 1..\n\nHundred-millions place: 8 (from first) +5 (from second) plus carry 1. 8+5=13, plus 1 is 14. So write down 4 and carry over 1. Wait, but there are no more digits left. So that carry 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning..\n\nPutting all the digits together from the end: the ones place was 6, then 0, 8,5,4,2,3,1,4, and the carried 1. Wait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any digits..\n\nWait, let me list each digit position properly, maybe I made a mistake in the order. Let's break down each number into individual digits:.\n\nFirst number: 8 3 3 9 3 1 6 9 1.\n\nSecond number:5 7 9 3 1 4 6 1 5.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1 +5 =6.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):9 +1=10 → 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):6 +6 +1=13 →3, carry 1.\n\nWait wait, hold on. Wait, the hundreds place: first number has 6, second number has 6? Wait, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, the first number is 833,931,691. Breaking down each digit:.\n\nFrom right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\n1:1 (ones).\n\n2:9 (tens).\n\n3:6 (hundreds).\n\n4:1 (thousands).\n\n5:3 (ten thousands).\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:3 (millions).\n\n8:3 (ten millions).\n\n9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:579,314,615:.\n\nFrom right to left:.\n\n1:5 (ones).\n\n2:1 (tens).\n\n3:6 (hundreds).\n\n4:4 (thousands).\n\n5:1 (ten thousands).\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:9 (millions).\n\n8:7 (ten millions).\n\n9:5 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the positions earlier. Let me list them properly:.\n\nFirst number digits (from ones to higher):.\n\nPosition 1:1.\n\nPosition 2:9.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition4:1.\n\nPosition5:3.\n\nPosition6:9.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition8:3.\n\nPosition9:8.\n\nSecond number:.\n\nPosition1:5.\n\nPosition2:1.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:1.\n\nPosition6:3.\n\nPosition7:9.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition9:5.\n\nNow adding each position with carries:.\n\nStart with position1 (ones):.\n\n1 +5 =6 → sum digit 6. Carry 0..\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n9 +1=10 → sum digit 0. Carry 1..\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n6 +6 +1 (carry) =13 → sum digit 3. Carry 1..\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n1 +4 +1=6 → sum digit6. Carry 0..\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n3 +1=4 → sum 4. Carry0..\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n9 +3=12 → sum 2. Carry1..\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n3 +9 +1=13 → sum3. Carry1..\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n3 +7 +1=11 → sum1. Carry1..\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n8 +5 +1=14 → sum4. Carry1..\n\nNow, since there are no more digits beyond position9, the carry1 is added as a new digit at the beginning..\n\nSo putting all sum digits from position1 to position9 and the final carry:.\n\nFinal digits:.\n\nCarry1 → 1, then position9 sum4, position8 sum1, position7 sum3, position6 sum2, position5 sum4, position4 sum6, position3 sum3, position2 sum0, position1 sum6..\n\nSo the total sum is 1 4 1 3 3 2 4 6 3 0 6 → wait, wait let me arrange them properly. The digits from left to right (highest to lowest place) would be:.\n\nCarry1 (billions place), then position9 sum4 (hundred millions), position8 sum1 (ten millions), position7 sum3 (millions), position6 sum2 (hundred thousands), position5 sum4 (ten thousands), position4 sum6 (thousands), position3 sum3 (hundreds), position2 sum0 (tens), position1 sum6 (ones)..\n\nSo writing that out: 1,413,324,6306? Wait, that can't be right. Wait, let's see:.\n\nWait, adding the digits:.\n\nThe carry1 is the first digit (billions place), followed by position9 sum4 (hundred millions), position8 sum1 (ten millions), position7 sum3 (millions), position6 sum2 (hundred thousands), position5 sum4 (ten thousands), position4 sum6 (thousands), position3 sum3 (hundreds), position2 sum0 (tens), position1 sum6 (ones). So the number would be 1,4 1 3, 3 2 4, 6 3 0 6? Wait, let's separate commas properly..\n\nWait, the places are:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nWait, no, wait. Wait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let's recount:.\n\nAfter adding, the digits are:.\n\nCarry1 (billions place) → 1.\n\nThen the sum of position9 (hundred millions place) →4.\n\nposition8 (ten millions) →1.\n\nposition7 (millions) →3.\n\nposition6 (hundred thousands) →2.\n\nposition5 (ten thousands) →4.\n\nposition4 (thousands) →6.\n\nposition3 (hundreds) →3.\n\nposition2 (tens) →0.\n\nposition1 (ones) →6.\n\nWait, no, position4 is thousands? Let me check the positions again..\n\nWait, in standard numbering, each position from right (ones) is:.\n\nPosition1: ones (10^0).\n\nPosition2: tens (10^1).\n\nPosition3: hundreds (10^2).\n\nPosition4: thousands (10^3).\n\nPosition5: ten thousands (10^4).\n\nPosition6: hundred thousands (10^5).\n\nPosition7: millions (10^6).\n\nPosition8: ten millions (10^7).\n\nPosition9: hundred millions (10^8).\n\nThen beyond that, position10 would be billions (10^9)..\n\nSo when we have a carryover from position9 (hundred millions), adding that carry1 would be in the billions place (position10)..\n\nSo the sum digits are:.\n\nBillions place (10^9):1.\n\nHundred millions (10^8):4.\n\nTen millions (10^7):1.\n\nMillions (10^6):3.\n\nHundred thousands (10^5):2.\n\nTen thousands (10^4):4.\n\nThousands (10^3):6.\n\nHundreds (10^2):3.\n\nTens (10^1):0.\n\nOnes (10^0):6.\n\nSo writing this out: 1,413,324,63606? Wait, that seems too long. Wait, maybe commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. Let me format it properly..\n\nStarting from the left:.\n\n1 (billions), then 413 (hundred millions, ten millions, millions), then 324 (hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands), then 636 (hundreds, tens, ones). Wait, no. Let's break down each digit:.\n\nDigits in order from highest to lowest place:.\n\n1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones)..\n\nSo combining these:.\n\n1,413,  million part: 1 (hundred millions) +413 million? Wait, I'm getting confused with the comma placement. Let's write each digit:.\n\n1 (B) 4 (HM) 1 (TM) 3 (M) 2 (HT) 4 (TTh) 6 (Th) 3 (H) 0 (T) 6 (O)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]  4%|3         | 19/500 [01:45<30:07,  3.76s/it]
