messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 883187487 and 106130128?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 883,187,487 and 106,130,128. Hmm, let's start by writing them down one under the other to line up the digits properly..\n\nSo, 883,187,487.\n+ 106,130,128.\n----------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The ones digits are 7 and 8. Adding those together, 7 + 8 equals 15. I write down 5 in the ones place and carry over 1 to the tens place..\n\nNext, the tens place: 8 + 2 is 10, plus the carried-over 1 makes 11. So that's 1 in the tens place and carry over 1 again..\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 4 + 1 is 5, plus the carryover 1 makes 6. No carryover here..\n\nThousands place: 7 + 0 is 7. Add the carried-over 0 (since there's nothing from the previous step), so it's 7..\n\nTen-thousands place: 8 + 3 is 11. Wait, hold on. Let me check that again. The first number has 8 in the ten-thousands place (883,187,487 breaks down as 8 8 3 1 8 7 4 8 7), actually, maybe I should break down each number's digits properly to avoid confusion..\n\nWait, maybe it's better to list each digit position by position. Let's do that..\n\nBreaking down 883,187,487:.\n\nDigits from right to left (ones to higher places):.\n7 (ones).\n8 (tens).\n4 (hundreds).\n7 (thousands).\n8 (ten thousands).\n1 (hundred thousands).\n3 (millions).\n8 (ten millions).\n8 (hundred millions).\n\nAnd 106,130,128:.\n\nDigits from right to left:.\n8 (ones).\n2 (tens).\n1 (hundreds).\n0 (thousands).\n3 (ten thousands).\n1 (hundred thousands).\n6 (millions).\n0 (ten millions).\n1 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, maybe aligning them properly:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits separated:.\n\nNumber1: 8 8 3 1 8 7 4 8 7.\n\nNumber2: 1 0 6 1 3 0 1 2 8.\n\nBut wait, actually, the numbers are:.\n\nNumber1: 883,187,487.\n\nBreaking down into places (from right, ones to left):.\n\nPositions: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands, millions, ten millions, hundred millions..\n\nSo:.\n\nNumber1:.\n\n- Ones: 7.\n- Tens: 8.\n- Hundreds: 4.\n- Thousands: 7.\n- Ten thousands: 8.\n- Hundred thousands: 1.\n- Millions: 3.\n- Ten millions: 8.\n- Hundred millions: 8.\n\nNumber2: 106,130,128.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n- Ones:8.\n- Tens:2.\n- Hundreds:1.\n- Thousands:0.\n- Ten thousands:3.\n- Hundred thousands:1.\n- Millions:6.\n- Ten millions:0.\n- Hundred millions:1.\n\nWait, wait. Let me confirm. Let's parse 106,130,128:.\n\nStarting from the right, digits are:.\n\n8 (ones).\n2 (tens).\n1 (hundreds).\n0 (thousands).\n3 (ten thousands).\n1 (hundred thousands).\n6 (millions).\n0 (ten millions).\n1 (hundred millions).\n\nYes, because 106,130,128 is 1 hundred million, 0 ten millions, 6 millions, 1 hundred thousand, 3 ten thousands, 0 thousands, 1 hundred, 2 tens, 8 ones..\n\nSo when adding the digits position by position:.\n\nStarting from ones place:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 7 (from number1) + 8 (number2) = 15. Write down 5, carry over 1..\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 8 (n1) + 2 (n2) = 10 + carryover 1 = 11. Write 1, carry over 1..\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):4 +1 =5 +1 (carry) =6. Write 6, no carryover..\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7 +0=7. No carryover. Write 7..\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8 +3=11. Write 1, carryover 1..\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 +1=2 +1 (carry)=3. Write 3..\n\nPosition7 (millions):3 +6=9. Write 9..\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8 +0=8. Write 8..\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +1=9. Write 9..\n\nPutting it all together from the highest digit to lowest: 9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),9 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),6 (hundreds),1 (tens),5 (ones). Wait, but let's reconstruct the sum properly..\n\nWait, after adding each position, the digits are:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones) to left:.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nTens:1.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:1 (with a carryover of 1 from previous step).\n\nWait, no. Let me retrace each step with carryover:.\n\n1. Ones place: 7 +8=15 → 5, carry 1..\n\n2. Tens:8+2=10 +1=11 →1, carry1..\n\n3. Hundreds:4+1=5 +1=6 →6, carry0..\n\n4. Thousands:7+0=7 →7, carry0..\n\n5. Ten thousands:8+3=11 →1, carry1..\n\nWait, wait, ten thousands place: number1 has 8 in the ten thousands place, number2 has 3 in ten thousands. So 8+3=11. So write 1, carryover 1..\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:1 (from number1) +1 (from number2) =2 + carryover 1=3. So 3, carry0..\n\n7. Millions place:3 (number1) +6 (number2)=9, carry0..\n\n8. Ten millions place:8 (n1) +0 (n2)=8, carry0..\n\n9. Hundred millions place:8 (n1)+1 (n2)=9, carry0..\n\nSo the sum digits from left to right (hundred millions to ones):.\n\n9 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),9 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),6 (hundreds),1 (tens),5 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 12%|#1        | 58/500 [03:29<19:34,  2.66s/it]
