messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 991244035 and 819735122?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 991,244,035 and 819,735,122. Hmm. Let me start by writing them down one under the other to make it easier..\n\n     991,244,035.\n   + 819,735,122.\n   ----------------.\n\nNow, I'll add each column starting from the ones place. .\n\nStarting with the ones digit: 5 + 2 is 7. So the ones place of the sum is 7. .\n\nNext, the tens place: 3 (from 991244035) + 2 (from 819735122) is 5. So tens digit is 5..\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 0 + 1 equals 1. That's straightforward..\n\nThousands place: 4 + 5. Wait, 4 + 5 is 9. Okay, so thousands digit is 9..\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 + 3. That's 7. So far, so good..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 + 7. 2 + 7 is 9. So that's 9..\n\nMillions place: 1 (from the first number) + 9 (from the second number). 1 + 9 is 10. Oh, here we have a carryover. Wait, so 10 million. So we write down 0 here and carry over 1 to the next digit..\n\nTen-millions place: 9 (from first number's ten-million place) + 1 (from the second number's ten-million place, which is 8's first digit? Wait, wait, let me check the numbers again. .\n\nWait, the first number is 991,244,035. Breaking it down: .\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (ones). .\n\nSecond number: 819,735,122. Breaking down: 8 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 2 (ones)..\n\nWait, so when adding the ten-millions place: first number has 9 (ten millions), second number has 1 (ten millions). So 9 + 1 = 10. But we also had a carryover of 1 from the millions place addition. Wait, no, let me step back. .\n\nWait, let me re-express both numbers with place values:.\n\nFirst number: 991,244,035.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions).\n- 9 (ten millions).\n- 1 (millions).\n- 2 (hundred thousands).\n- 4 (ten thousands).\n- 4 (thousands).\n- 0 (hundreds).\n- 3 (tens).\n- 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 819,735,122.\n\n- 8 (hundred millions).\n- 1 (ten millions).\n- 9 (millions).\n- 7 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 5 (thousands).\n- 1 (hundreds).\n- 2 (tens).\n- 2 (ones).\n\nSo when adding each place:.\n\nStarting from ones:.\n\n5 (ones) + 2 (ones) = 7. No carryover..\n\nTens: 3 + 2 = 5. No carryover..\n\nHundreds: 0 +1 =1. No carryover..\n\nThousands: 4 +5 =9. No carryover..\n\nTen thousands:4 +3=7. No carryover..\n\nHundred thousands:2 +7=9. No carryover..\n\nMillions place:1 (from first number) +9 (from second number). 1+9=10. So we write 0 here and carry over 1..\n\nTen millions place:9 (from first) +1 (from second) =10, plus the carryover 1 makes 11. So write 1 here and carry over 1 again..\n\nHundred millions place:9 (from first) +8 (from second) =17, plus the carryover 1 makes 18. So write 8 here and carry over 1 to the next place, which is billions..\n\nBut since both numbers are 9-digit, the next digit (billions place) would be that carryover of 1..\n\nPutting it all together, the sum would be 1 (billions), 8 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 7 (ones). Wait, wait, let me check again..\n\nWait, when adding:.\n\nHundred millions place: 9 +8 =17, plus carryover 1 =18. So write 8 here, carryover 1 to billions. Then, the sum would start with 1 (billions), 8 (hundred millions), then the ten millions place had 9+1+1=11. Wait, let's go step by step..\n\nLet me write each digit step by step with carryover..\n\nOnes: 5+2=7, carryover 0..\n\nTens:3+2=5, carryover 0..\n\nHundreds:0+1=1, carryover 0..\n\nThousands:4+5=9, carryover 0..\n\nTen thousands:4+3=7, carryover 0..\n\nHundred thousands:2+7=9, carryover 0..\n\nMillions:1 (from first) +9 (from second) =10. Write 0 here, carryover 1..\n\nTen millions:9 +1 =10, plus carryover 1 =11. Write 1 here, carryover 1..\n\nHundred millions:9 +8 =17, plus carryover 1 =18. Write 8 here, carryover 1..\n\nBillions place: that carryover 1..\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\n1 (billions), 8 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),1 (hundreds),5 (tens),7 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 14%|#3        | 68/500 [04:03<20:28,  2.84s/it] 14%|#3        | 69/500 [04:06<19:57,  2.78s/it]
