7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 0 (ones)..\n\nSo when adding digit by digit:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 5 + 0 = 5..\n\nTens: 0 + 7 = 7..\n\nHundreds: 1 + 2 = 3..\n\nThousands: 4 + 5 = 9..\n\nTen-thousands: 9 + 6 = 15. Write down 5, carry over 1..\n\nHundred-thousands: 0 + 1 = 1, plus the carried 1 makes 2..\n\nMillions place: 4 + 7 = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1..\n\nTen millions place: 8 + 5 = 13, plus the carried 1 makes 14. Write down 4, carry over 1..\n\nHundred millions place: 3 + 9 = 12, plus the carried 1 makes 13. Write down 3, carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, that carried 1 becomes a new digit at the billions place..\n\nPutting it all together: 1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
