messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 750310277 and 563486610?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 750,310,277 and 563,486,610. Hmm, adding two large numbers. Let me start by writing them down one under the other, aligning the digits properly..\n\nSo, 750,310,277.\nplus 563,486,610.\n________________.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. .\n\nFirst, the ones place: 7 + 0 equals 7. That's straightforward. So the ones digit of the sum is 7..\n\nNext, the tens place: 7 (from the first number) plus 1 (from the second number). 7 + 1 is 8. So the tens digit is 8..\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 2 (from 750,310,277) plus 6 (from 563,486,610). 2 + 6 is 8. So hundreds place is 8..\n\nThousands place: 0 (from first number) plus 6 (from second number). 0 + 6 = 6. Thousands digit is 6..\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 (from first) plus 8 (from second). 1 + 8 is 9. So ten-thousands digit is 9..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 (from first) plus 4 (from second). 3 + 4 = 7. So that's 7..\n\nMillions place: 0 (from first) plus 3 (from second). 0 + 3 = 3. Millions digit is 3..\n\nTen-millions place: 5 (from first) plus 6 (from second). 5 + 6 is 11. Oh wait, here I might have a carry-over. Wait, no, actually, let's check the positions again. Wait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me verify the numbers again..\n\nWait, the first number is 750,310,277. Let me break it down:.\n\nDigits (from right to left):.\n7 (ones), 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 0 (thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (millions), 5 (ten millions), 7 (hundred millions)..\n\nWait, maybe I should write them out:.\n\n750,310,277:.\n- Hundreds of millions: 7.\n- Ten millions:5.\n- Millions:0.\n- Hundred thousands:3.\n- Ten thousands:1.\n- Thousands:0.\n- Hundreds:2.\n- Tens:7.\n- Ones:7.\n\nSimilarly, 563,486,610:.\n- Hundreds of millions:5.\n- Ten millions:6.\n- Millions:3.\n- Hundred thousands:4.\n- Ten thousands:8.\n- Thousands:6.\n- Hundreds:6.\n- Tens:1.\n- Ones:0.\n\nWait, so when adding the ten millions place: 5 (from first number's ten millions) + 6 (from second number's ten millions) = 11. So that would be 1 carry-over to the hundred millions place. So the ten millions digit is 1 and carryover 1..\n\nThen, the hundred millions place: 7 (from first) +5 (from second) =12, plus the carryover 1 makes 13. So the hundred millions digit is 3 and carryover 1 to the next place (which would be billions, but since both numbers are 9-digit, the total would be up to 10 digits if there's a carryover here)..\n\nWait, but I think I might have messed up the places. Let me try aligning them properly:.\n\n          7 5 0 3 1 0 2 7 7.\n        + 5 6 3 4 8 6 6 1 0.\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 7 + 0 =7..\n\nTens:7 +1=8..\n\nHundreds:2 +6=8..\n\nThousands:0 +6=6..\n\nTen thousands:1 +8=9..\n\nHundred thousands:3 +4=7..\n\nMillions:0 +3=3..\n\nTen millions:5 +6=11. So write down 1 and carryover 1..\n\nHundred millions:7 +5=12, plus the carryover 1 is 13. So write down 3 and carryover 1..\n\nNow, since there's a carryover here, we add a new digit at the billions place: 1..\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the billions place:1, then 3, 1, 3, 7, 9, 6, 8, 8,7..\n\nWait, let's check each digit again step by step with carryover:.\n\nStarting from ones:.\n\n1. Ones place: 7 + 0 =7. No carryover. Sum digit:7..\n\n2. Tens place:7 +1=8. Sum digit:8. No carryover..\n\n3. Hundreds:2 +6=8. Sum digit:8. No carryover..\n\n4. Thousands:0 +6=6. Sum digit:6. No carryover..\n\n5. Ten thousands:1 +8=9. Sum digit:9. No carryover..\n\n6. Hundred thousands:3 +4=7. Sum digit:7. No carryover..\n\n7. Millions place:0 +3=3. Sum digit:3. No carryover..\n\n8. Ten millions place:5 +6=11. Write down 1, carryover 1..\n\n9. Hundred millions place:7 +5=12. Plus carryover 1=13. Write down 3, carryover 1..\n\n10. Since we're at the highest place now, the carryover 1 becomes the next digit: billions place:1..\n\nSo the sum is 1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions carried over?), wait no. Wait, let's structure it properly:.\n\nThe digits from right to left (after addition):.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nTens:8.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:1 (from 5+6=11, so digit 1 with carryover 1).\n\nHundred millions:3 (from 7+5=12 +1=13, so digit 3 with carryover 1).\n\nThen the carryover 1 goes to billions place:1..\n\nSo the full number is 1,3 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),3 (millions?), wait no. Wait, maybe I should list the digits in order..\n\nWait, the sum is:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:3 (from 7+5+carryover).\n\nTen millions:1 (from 5+6=11, wrote 1, carried 1).\n\nMillions:3 (from 0+3=3).\n\nHundred thousands:7 (3+4).\n\nTen thousands:9 (1+8).\n\nThousands:6 (0+6).\n\nHundreds:8 (2+6).\n\nTens:8 (7+1).\n\nOnes:7 (7+0).\n\nWait, that seems mixed up. Let me try to write each digit place properly:.\n\nAfter addition:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nTens:8.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:1 (with a carryover from here).\n\nHundred millions:3 (plus carryover made it 13, so 3 here and carryover 1).\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo putting all together, from left to right (billions to ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 19%|#8        | 93/500 [05:20<24:02,  3.55s/it]
