rectly..\n\nFirst number: 697,954,550  .\nDigits (from right to left):  .\n0 (ones), 5 (tens), 5 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 6 (hundred millions)..\n\nSecond number: 104,161,451  .\nDigits:  .\n1 (ones), 5 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions)..\n\nWait, that's not right. Let me correct that. The second number is 104,161,451. Breaking it down from right to left:.\n\n1 (ones), 5 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions). Yes, because 104,161,451 is 1 hundred million, 0 ten millions, 4 millions, 1 hundred thousand, 6 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 4 hundreds, 5 tens, 1 ones..\n\nSo when adding each corresponding digit:.\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\n1. Ones place: 0 + 1 = 1. Write 1. No carryover..\n2. Tens place: 5 + 5 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n3. Hundreds place: 5 + 4 = 9, plus carryover 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n4. Thousands place: 4 + 1 = 5, plus carryover 1 = 6. Write 6. No carryover..\n5. Ten thousands place: 5 + 6 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1..\n6. Hundred thousands place: 9 + 1 = 10, plus carryover 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1..\n7. Millions place: 7 + 4 = 11, plus carryover 1 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1..\n8. Ten millions place: 9 + 0 = 9, plus carryover 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n9. Hundred millions place: 6 + 1 = 7, plus carryover 1 = 8. Write 8..\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left (after addition):  .\nOnes:1  .\nTens:0  .\nHundreds:0  .\nThousands:6  .\nTen thousands:1  .\nHundred thousands:1  .\nMillions:2  .\nTen millions:0  .\nHundred millions:8  .\n\nWait, but let's assemble the number properly. Let me list the digits from the highest place to the lowest..\n\nHundred millions:8  .\nTen millions:0  .\nMillions:2  .\nHundred thousands:1  .\nTen thousands:1  .\nThousands:6  