messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 882092648 and 648639351?\n# Reasoning:", '<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. I need to add 882,092,648 and 648,639,351. Hmm, adding big numbers can be tricky, but I\'ll take it step by step..\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has an 8 in the ones place, and the second number has a 1. So 8 + 1 equals 9. That\'s straightforward. So the ones digit of the sum is 9. Wait, the template mentioned "ones place: <<digit1>>" so maybe I need to write that as 8 + 1 = 9, and carry over 0? But 8 + 1 is 9, no carry. .\n\nNext, moving to the tens place. The first number has 4 in the tens place, and the second number has 5. So 4 + 5 = 9. Again, that\'s 9 in the tens place, no carry. .\n\nThen the hundreds place: 6 (from first number) + 3 (from second) equals 9. So hundreds digit is 9. .\n\nThousands place: 2 (from first number) + 9 (from second). Wait, 2 + 9 is 11. Oh, so here we have to carry over 1. So the thousands digit is 1, and carry over 1 to the next place. .\n\nThen the ten-thousands place: 9 (from first) + 3 (from second) plus the carried 1. 9 + 3 is 12, plus 1 is 13. So that\'s 3 in the ten-thousands place, carry over 1. .\n\nHundred-thousands place: 0 (first number) + 6 (second) + 1 (carry). 0 +6 is 6, plus 1 is 7. No carry here. .\n\nMillions place: 8 (first) and 8 (second). 8+8=16. So that\'s 6 in the millions place, carry over 1. .\n\nTen-millions place: 8 (first) +4 (second) +1 (carry). 8+4=12, plus 1 is 13. So 3 here, carry 1. .\n\nHundred-millions place: 8 (first) +6 (second) +1 (carry). 8+6=14, plus 1 is 15. So 5 here, carry 1. .\n\nFinally, the billions place: there\'s a carryover of 1, so that\'s 1. .\n\nPutting it all together: 1 (billions), 5 (hundred-millions), 3 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 7 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (ones). Wait, let me check again..\n\nWait, let me line them up properly to avoid confusion:.\n\nFirst number: 882,092,648.\nSecond number: 648,639,351.\n\nAligning by digits:.\n\nBillions: 8 vs 6? Wait, actually both numbers are 9 digits, so:.\n\nBreaking down each number digit by digit:.\n\n882092648:.\n- 8 (hundred millions).\n- 8 (ten millions).\n- 2 (millions).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 9 (ten thousands).\n- 2 (thousands).\n- 6 (hundreds).\n- 4 (tens).\n- 8 (ones).\n\n648639351:.\n- 6 (hundred millions).\n- 4 (ten millions).\n- 8 (millions).\n- 6 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 3 (hundreds).\n- 5 (tens).\n- 1 (ones).\n\nAdding each digit starting from ones:.\n\nOnes: 8 +1 =9.\nTens:4 +5=9.\nHundreds:6 +3=9.\nThousands:2 +9=11 (write 1, carry 1).\nTen thousands:9 +3=12 +1 (carry)=13 (write 3, carry 1).\nHundred thousands:0 +6=6 +1 (carry)=7.\nMillions:2 +8=10 (wait, wait, millions digit? Wait, the first number\'s millions digit is 2, second\'s is 8. So 2+8=10. So write 0, carry 1. Wait, but wait, the millions place here. Let me check again..\n\nWait, the first number is 882,092,648. Let\'s write it as:.\n\nHundred millions:8.\nTen millions:8.\nMillions:2.\nHundred thousands:0.\nTen thousands:9.\nThousands:2.\nHundreds:6.\nTens:4.\nOnes:8.\n\nSecond number:648,639,351:.\n\nHundred millions:6.\nTen millions:4.\nMillions:8.\nHundred thousands:6.\nTen thousands:3.\nThousands:9.\nHundreds:3.\nTens:5.\nOnes:1.\n\nSo when adding, the digits positions are:.\n\n- Hundred millions: 8 +6 =14 (but wait, need to check the carry from previous digit).\n\nWait, let me do it step by step properly with all carries:.\n\nStart from ones:.\n\n1. Ones place: 8 +1 =9. No carry..\n\n2. Tens:4 +5=9. No carry..\n\n3. Hundreds:6 +3=9. No carry..\n\n4. Thousands:2 +9=11. Write down 1, carry over 1..\n\n5. Ten thousands:9 +3=12 +1 (carry)=13. Write down 3, carry 1..\n\n6. Hundred thousands:0 +6=6 +1 (carry)=7. No carry..\n\n7. Millions:2 +8=10. Write 0, carry 1..\n\n8. Ten millions:8 +4=12 +1 (carry)=13. Write 3, carry 1..\n\n9. Hundred millions:8 +6=14 +1 (carry)=15. Write 5, carry 1..\n\nNow, since we\'re at the hundred millions place, the next is billions. The carryover 1 from hundred millions becomes 1 in the billions place..\n\nSo the sum is:.\n\n1 (billions), 5 (hundred millions),3 (ten millions),0 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),9 (hundreds),9 (tens),9 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is ']] 22%|##2       | 110/500 [06:05<17:53,  2.75s/it]
