\n0 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 883,994,985:.\n\n8 (hundred millions).\n8 (ten millions).\n3 (millions).\n9 (hundred thousands).\n9 (ten thousands).\n4 (thousands).\n9 (hundreds).\n8 (tens).\n5 (ones).\n\nSo when adding the hundred-millions place: 3 + 8 = 11, but wait, the second number has 8 in the hundred millions place. So 3 + 8 = 11. Then plus any carry? Let's retrace the steps:.\n\nStarting from ones:.\n\n0 +5=5 (no carry).\n1 +8=9 (no carry).\n6+9=15 (write 5, carry 1).\n8+4=12 +1=13 (write 3, carry1).\n4+9=13 +1=14 (write4, carry1).\n2+9=11 +1=12 (write2, carry1).\n1+3=4 +1=5 (no carry).\n7+8=15 (write5, carry1).\n3+8=11 +1=12 (write2, carry1, but there are no higher places, so that carry becomes a new digit at the start).\n\nWait, but when adding the hundred-millions place: 3 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 11, plus the carryover from the ten-millions place, which was 1. So 11 +1=12. So the hundred-millions place becomes 2, and carry over 1 to the next higher place, which is the billions place. Since there are no digits beyond that, we add the 1 as a new digit. So the sum should start with 1, then 2, etc..\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nTens:9.\n\nHundreds:5 (carry 1).\n\nThousands:3 (carry1).\n\nTen-thousands:4 (carry1).\n\nHundred-thousands:2 (carry1).\n\nMillions:5 (no carry).\n\nTen-millions:5 (carry1).\n\nHundred-millions:2 (carry1).\n\nThen the carry 1 becomes a new digit:1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
