ly to avoid confusion. Let's see:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\nNumber1: ... 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (ones).\nNumber2: ... 4 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, when written out:.\n\n207,512,855 is:.\nDigits (from right to left, positions ones to hundred millions):.\nOnes:5, Tens:5, Hundreds:8, Thousands:2, Ten thousands:1, Hundred thousands:5, Millions:7, Ten millions:0, Hundred millions:2.\n\nSimilarly, 322,924,497 is:.\nOnes:7, Tens:9, Hundreds:4, Thousands:4, Ten thousands:2, Hundred thousands:9, Millions:2, Ten millions:2, Hundred millions:3.\n\nSo adding digit by digit:.\n\nStarting with ones place:.\n\n1. Ones: 5 +7 =12 → write 2, carryover 1..\n\n2. Tens:5 +9 =14 +1=15 → write 5, carryover 1..\n\n3. Hundreds:8 +4=12 +1=13 → write 3, carryover 1..\n\n4. Thousands:2 +4=6 +1=7 → write 7, carryover 0..\n\n5. Ten thousands:1 +2=3 +0=3 → write 3, carryover 0..\n\n6. Hundred thousands:5 +9=14 → write 4, carryover 1..\n\n7. Millions:7 +2=9 +1=10 → write 0, carryover 1..\n\n8. Ten millions:0 +2=2 +1=3 → write 3, carryover 0..\n\n9. Hundred millions:2 +3=5 → write 5</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
