o carry-over..\n\nHundred-millions place: 3 (from first number) and 8 (from second). 3+8=11. Since these are the highest digits, we just write 11 here. Wait, but when adding, the hundred-millions place would be 3 + 8 =11, but since there's no higher place, we just put 11 in front. .\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left (after each step), the sum would be:.\n\nStarting with ones: 4.\n\nTens: 5 (from 15, carry 1).\n\nHundreds: 6.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nTen-thousands:5 (carry 1).\n\nHundred-thousands:6 (carry 1).\n\nMillions:4 (carry 1).\n\nTen-millions:7 (carry 0).\n\nHundred-millions:11 (since no higher place, just 11 here).\n\nSo combining all these digits: 11 (hundred-millions), 7 (ten-millions), 4 (millions), 6 (hundred-thousands),5 (ten-thousands),1 (thousands),6 (hundreds),5 (tens),4 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
