(hundred-millions).\n\nSo when adding each column:.\n\n1. Ones place: 6 + 8 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1..\n2. Tens place: 4 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 8. No carry here..\n3. Hundreds place: 2 + 9 = 11. Write down 1, carry over 1..\n4. Thousands place: 5 + 8 + 1 = 14. Write 4, carry 1..\n5. Ten-thousands place: 3 + 8 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1..\n6. Hundred-thousands place: 2 + 9 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1..\n7. Millions place: 9 + 2 + 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1..\n8. Ten-millions place: 7 + 9 + 1 = 17. Write 7, carry 1..\n9. Hundred-millions place: 4 + 5 + 1 = 10. Write 0, carry 1 (but since there are no higher digits, that carry becomes a new digit at the beginning)..\n\nWait, but let me verify each step again carefully to avoid mistakes..\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\n1. Ones: 6 + 8 = 14. So 4, carry 1. Correct..\n2. Tens: 4 (from first number) + 3 (second number) + 1 (carry) = 8. Yes, write 8. No carry..\n3. Hundreds: 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carry 1. Correct..\n4. Thousands: 5 + 8 = 13, plus the carry 1 makes 14. So write 4, carry 1. Right..\n5. Ten-thousands: 3 + 8 = 11, plus carry 1 is 12. Write 2, carry 1..\n6. Hundred-thousands: 2 (from first number) + 9 (second) = 11, plus carry 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1..\n7. Millions place: 9 (first) + 2 (second) = 11, plus carry 1 = 12. Write 2, carry 1..\n8. Ten-millions: 7 (first) + 9 (second) = 16, plus carry 1 = 17. Write 7, carry 1..\n9. Hundred-millions: 4 (first) +5 (second) =9, plus carry 1 =10. Write 0, carry 1. But since there are no higher places, that 1 becomes a new digit at the start..\n\nSo putting all the digits together from the results:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost (ones) to left:.\n\n4 (ones), 8 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 2 (ten-thousands), 2 (hundred-thousands), 2 (millions), 7 (ten-millions), 0 (hundred-millions), and the final carry 1 becomes the next digit: 1 (billions place?)..\n\nWait, but wait. Let's assemble the sum step by step. Each step's written digit is added from right to left..\n\nSo the digits we have after each step are:.\n\n1. Ones: 4.\n2. Tens: 8.\n3. Hundreds:1.\n4. Thousands:4.\n5. Ten-thousands:2.\n6. Hundred-thousands:2.\n7. Millions:2.\n8. Ten-millions:7.\n9. Hundred-millions:0.\n10. The carry-over 1 at the end becomes 1 in the billions place..\n\nSo combining all these digits from left to right (highest to lowest place):.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred-millions),7 (ten-millions), 2 (millions), 2 (hundred-thousands),2 (ten-thousands),4 (thousands),1 (hundreds),8 (tens),4 (ones). .\n\nWait, that can't be right. Wait, let's list the digits in order from highest place to lowest. The original numbers are 479,325,246 (which is 479 million) and 592,988,938 (592 million). Adding them, we expect around 1,072 million (1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 27%|##7       | 135/500 [07:14<19:39,  3.23s/it]
