millions), 4 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 9 (hundred-thousands), 2 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, let me check that again. Wait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let me recount each digit step by step to make sure..\n\nWait, maybe I should list each digit position properly:.\n\nLet's write out each number's digits:.\n\nFirst number (509,314,931):.\nDigits from right to left (ones to billions):.\n1 (ones), 3 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 5 (hundred millions)..\n\nSecond number (837,106,430):.\nDigits from right to left:.\n0 (ones), 3 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions)..\n\nAdding each position:.\n\nOnes: 1 + 0 = 1.\nTens: 3 + 3 = 6.\nHundreds: 9 + 4 = 13 → write 3, carry 1.\nThousands: 4 + 6 + 1 (carry) = 11 → write 1, carry 1.\nTen thousands: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2.\nHundred thousands: 3 + 6 = 9.\nMillions: 9 + 7 = 16 → write 6, carry 1.\nTen millions: 0 + 3 + 1 = 4.\nHundred millions:5 +8 =13 → write 3, carry 1.\nBillions place: carry 1..\n\nSo the sum from right to left (ones to billions) is:.\n\n1 (ones), 6 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 4 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions), 1 (billions)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
