s 2.\nMillions: 1 vs 4.\nHundred thousands: 2 vs 0.\nTen thousands:8 vs9.\nThousands:8 vs6.\nHundreds:2 vs6.\nTens:6 vs2.\nOnes:4 vs6.\n\nWait, but when adding digit by digit, starting from the ones:.\n\nOnes: 4 +6 =10 → 0, carry 1..\n\nTens:6 +2=8 +1=9 → 9, no carry..\n\nHundreds:2 +6=8 →8, no carry..\n\nThousands:8 +6=14 →4, carry 1..\n\nTen thousands:8 +9=17 +1=18 →8, carry 1..\n\nHundred thousands:2 +0=2 +1=3 →3, no carry..\n\nMillions:1 +4=5 →5, no carry..\n\nTen millions:9 +2=11 →1, carry 1..\n\nHundred millions:2 +9=11 +1=12 →2, carry 1..\n\nBut wait, after hundred millions, there's the billions place. Since both numbers are 9-digit, the carryover here would add a new digit. So the final sum would start with 1 (from the carryover), then 2, etc..\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStart from ones:.\n\n0.\n\nTens:9.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:4 (carry 1).\n\nTen thousands:8 (carry 1).\n\nHundred thousands:3 (carry 0).\n\nMillions:5 (carry 0).\n\nTen millions:1 (carry 1).\n\nHundred millions:2 (carry 1).\n\nThen the next digit (billions place): 1 from the carryover</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
