).\n- 2 (tens).\n- 9 (ones).\n\nSo when aligned by place:.\n\nMillions, etc. Wait, maybe it's easier to write them digit by digit from right to left (ones to the highest place):.\n\nFirst number digits (right to left):.\n\n1 (ones).\n3 (tens).\n6 (hundreds).\n0 (thousands).\n6 (ten thousands).\n9 (hundred thousands).\n7 (millions).\n9 (ten millions).\n9 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number digits (right to left):.\n\n9 (ones).\n2 (tens).\n2 (hundreds).\n3 (thousands).\n4 (ten thousands).\n6 (hundred thousands).\n3 (millions).\n6 (ten millions).\n4 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, so aligning each position:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 1 +9 =10, carryover 1..\n\nPosition 2 (tens):3 +2=5 +1=6, carryover 0..\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6 +2=8, no carryover..\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0 +3=3, no carryover..\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 +4=10, so digit 0, carryover 1..\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9 +6=15 +1 (carryover)=16. So digit 6, carryover 1..\n\nPosition7 (millions):7 +3=10 +1=11. Digit 1, carryover 1..\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9 +6=15 +1=16. Digit 6, carryover 1..\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9 +4=13 +1=14. Digit 4, carryover 1..\n\nNow, since there are no more digits beyond hundred millions, the carryover 1 would create a new digit at the billions place: 1..\n\nPutting it all together from the highest digit to the lowest:.\n\nThe digits we have are:.\n\nFrom position9 (hundred millions):14 → 4 with carryover 1..\n\nThen the carryover 1 becomes the billions place: 1..\n\nSo the result is 1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones)..\n\nWait, let's check each position again step by step to make sure..\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n1. Ones: 1 +9 =10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n\n2. Tens: 3 +2=5 +1=6. Write 6, no carryover..\n\n3. Hundreds:6 +2=8. Write 8, no carryover..\n\n4. Thousands:0 +3=3. Write 3..\n\n5. Ten thousands:6 +4=10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n\n6. Hundred thousands:9 +6=15. Plus carryover 1 makes 16. Write 6, carryover 1..\n\n7. Millions:7 +3=10. Plus carryover 1=11. Write 1, carryover 1..\n\n8. Ten millions:9 +6=15. Plus carryover 1=16. Write 6, carryover 1..\n\n9. Hundred millions:9 +4=13. Plus carryover 1=14. Write 4, carryover 1..\n\n10. Billions: carryover 1. Write 1..\n\nSo putting all digits together from left to right (billions to ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 33%|###3      | 167/500 [08:47<14:17,  2.57s/it]
