3, and the second number is 553,244,221, so its hundred-millions place is 5. Adding those gives 8. So that's correct..\n\nPutting all the digits together from each step: starting from the right, we have 2 in ones, 5 in tens, 0 in hundreds (with a carry-over), then 6 in thousands, 1 in ten-thousands (with carry-over), 8 in hundred-thousands, 5 in millions, 8 in ten-millions, and 8 in hundred-millions. Wait, but let me verify each digit again to make sure I didn't mix up any places..\n\nWait, let's list all digits properly:.\n\nFirst number: 3 3 2 5 7 1 8 3 1.\n\nSecond number:5 5 3 2 4 4 2 2 1.\n\nAdding each column from right to left:.\n\n1. Ones: 1 +1 =2.\n\n2. Tens:3+2=5.\n\n3. Hundreds:8+2=10 → write 0, carry 1.\n\n4. Thousands:1 (carry) +1 +4=6.\n\n5. Ten-thousands:7 +4=11 → write 1, carry 1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands:5 +2 +1 (carry)=8.\n\n7. Millions:2 +3=5.\n\n8. Ten-millions:3 +5=8.\n\n9. Hundred-millions:3 +5=8.\n\nSo combining all the digits written: 8 (hundred millions) 8 (ten millions) 5 (millions) 8 (hundred thousands) 1 (ten thousands) 6 (thousands) 0 (hundreds) 5 (tens) 2 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
