rry over 1. But wait, since these are the hundred-millions places, adding them plus the carry-over. Wait, let me check again. .\n\nWait, the first number is 646,824,528. Breaking it down: 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 6 (millions), then 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 8 (ones)..\n\nThe second number is 814,304,008: 8 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (ones)..\n\nSo when adding the hundred-millions place: 6 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 14. So we write 4 here and carry over 1 to the next higher place, which would be the billions place. But since both numbers are 9-digit, the sum might be a 10-digit number. So the billions place would be 1 from the carry-over..\n\nPutting it all together: starting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes: 6.\nTens: 3.\nHundreds:5.\nThousands:8.\nTen-thousands:2.\nHundred-thousands:1 (since 8+3=11, write 1 carry 1).\nMillions:1 (6+4=10 plus carry 1 makes 11, write 1 carry 1).\nTen-millions:6 (4+1+1=6).\nHundred-millions:4 (6+8=14, write 4 carry 1).\nBillions:1 (carry over 1)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
