usand).\n- 0 (ten thousand).\n- 2 (thousand).\n- 4 (hundred).\n- 1 (ten).\n- 6 (one).\n\nSecond number: 349,230,377.\nBreakdown:.\n- 3 (hundred million).\n- 4 (ten million).\n- 9 (million).\n- 2 (hundred thousand).\n- 3 (ten thousand).\n- 0 (thousand).\n- 3 (hundred).\n- 7 (ten).\n- 7 (one).\n\nWait, I think I messed up the places earlier. Let me re-align them properly:.\n\nHundred million: 8 vs 3.\nTen million: 9 vs 4.\nMillion: 4 vs 9.\nHundred thousand: 4 vs 2.\nTen thousand: 0 vs 3.\nThousand: 2 vs 0.\nHundred: 4 vs 3.\nTen: 1 vs 7.\nOne:6 vs7.\n\nSo adding each corresponding place:.\n\nStarting from the ones place (rightmost):.\n\nOne: 6 +7 =13 → 3, carryover 1..\n\nTen: 1 +7 +1 (carry) =9 → 9, no carryover..\n\nHundred:4 +3 =7 →7..\n\nThousand:2 +0 =2..\n\nWait, wait, wait. Wait, the thousand place in the first number is 2, and the second number's thousand place is 0. So 2 +0=2. Then ten thousand place: 0 (first number) +3 (second number) =3. Hundred thousand:4 +2=6. Million place:4 +9=13 →3, carryover 1. Ten million place:9 +4=13 plus carryover 1 makes 14 →4, carryover 1. Hundred million place:8 +3=11 plus carryover 1=12. .\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nOnes:3.\n\nTens:9.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nMillions:3 (with a carryover from million place).\n\nWait, let me step through each place again carefully, maybe I made a mistake in the higher digits..\n\nLet me list each digit position:.\n\n1. Ones place: 6 +7 =13 →3, carry 1..\n2. Tens place:1 +7 =8 +1 (carry) =9 →9, carry 0..\n3. Hundreds place:4 +3=7 →7, carry 0..\n4. Thousands place:2 +0=2 →2, carry 0..\n5. Ten thousands place:0 +3=3 →3, carry 0..\n6. Hundred thousands place:4 +2=6 →6, carry 0..\n7. Millions place:4 +9=13 →3, carry 1..\n8. Ten millions place:9 +4=13 +1 (carry) =14 →4, carry 1..\n9. Hundred millions place:8 +3=11 +1 (carry) =12 →2, carry 1. But since we're at the highest place, the carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning..\n\nSo putting all the digits together with the carryover:.\n\nThe sum would be 1 (from the final carryover) followed by 2 (hundred million),4 (ten million),3 (million),6 (hundred thousand),3 (ten thousand),2 (thousand),7 (hundred),9 (ten),3 (one)..\n\nWait, let's check:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit:.\n\nAfter adding hundred millions place: 8 +3=11, plus carryover 1 from ten millions place (since ten millions place had 9+4=13 +1=14, which carried over 1). Wait, no. Wait, the carryover from the million place was 1. Let me re-examine:.\n\nWait, the million place is 4 (from first number) and 9 (from second number). Adding those gives 13. So million place digit is 3, carryover 1 to the ten million place..\n\nThen ten million place:9 (from first number) +4 (from second number) =13, plus carryover 1 gives 14. So ten million place digit is 4, carryover 1 to hundred million place..\n\nHundred million place:8 (first number) +3 (second number) =11, plus carryover 1 gives 12. So hundred million place is 2, carryover 1. Since there are no higher places, this carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning: 1, then 2, then the rest..\n\nSo the digits from highest to lowest:.\n\n1 (carryover) → 2 (hundred million) →4 (ten million) →3 (million) →6 (hundred thousand) →3 (ten thousand) →2 (thousand) →7 (hundred) →9 (ten) →3 (one)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 36%|###6      | 181/500 [09:27<14:15,  2.68s/it] 36%|###6      | 182/500 [09:29<14:09,  2.67s/it]
