messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 967099234 and 125931556?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out how to add these two numbers: 967,099,234 and 125,931,556. Hmm, adding large numbers can be a bit tricky, but I remember that you just need to line them up by place value and add each column starting from the ones place. Let me write them down to make sure they're aligned properly..\n\nFirst, let's write the numbers one on top of the other:.\n\n  967,099,234.\n+ 125,931,556.\n----------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The ones digits are 4 and 6. Adding those together: 4 + 6 = 10. Oh, so that gives me 0 in the ones place and I carry over 1 to the tens place..\n\nNext, the tens place: 3 + 5 = 8, plus the carried over 1 makes 9. So the tens digit is 9, no carry-over here..\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 2 + 5 = 7. Adding the carried over 0 (since there was no carry from the previous step), so the hundreds place is 7..\n\nThousands place: 9 + 1 = 10. Wait, the thousands digits are 9 (from the first number) and 1 (from the second). Adding them gives 10. So we put 0 here and carry over 1..\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 + 3 = 12, plus the carried 1 makes 13. So we write down 3 and carry over 1..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 0 (from the first number) + 9 (from the second) = 9, plus the carryover 1 equals 10. So we put 0 here and carry over 1 again..\n\nMillions place: 7 (from the first number) + 5 (from the second) = 12, plus the carryover 1 makes 13. Write down 3, carry over 1..\n\nTen-millions place: 6 (from the first number) + 2 (from the second) = 8, plus the carryover 1 equals 9. So that's 9 here..\n\nHundred-millions place: 9 (from the first number) + 1 (from the second) = 10. So we put 0 here and carry over 1. Wait, but there's no higher place, so that carryover 1 would become a new digit at the beginning. So the final number would start with 1..\n\nPutting all the digits together, starting from the highest place: 1 (from the last carryover), then 0 (from the hundred-millions place), then 9 (ten-millions), 3 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, let me check again because that doesn't seem right. Let me recount each step carefully to make sure I didn't make a mistake..\n\nWait, when adding the millions place: the first number has 7 in the millions place (since it's 967,099,234 – the millions digit is the third from the left: 967, so 7 is in the millions place?), actually, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me clarify each digit's place..\n\nBreaking down 967,099,234:.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions).\n- 6 (ten millions).\n- 7 (millions).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 9 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 2 (hundreds).\n- 3 (tens).\n- 4 (ones).\n\nAnd 125,931,556:.\n\n- 1 (hundred millions).\n- 2 (ten millions).\n- 5 (millions).\n- 9 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 1 (thousands).\n- 5 (hundreds).\n- 5 (tens).\n- 6 (ones).\n\nNow, aligning them:.\n\nHundred millions: 9 vs 1.\nTen millions: 6 vs 2.\nMillions:7 vs5.\nHundred thousands:0 vs9.\nTen thousands:9 vs3.\nThousands:9 vs1.\nHundreds:2 vs5.\nTens:3 vs5.\nOnes:4 vs6.\n\nAdding each column from right to left:.\n\nOnes:4+6=10 → 0, carry 1.\nTens:3+5=8 +1=9 → 9, carry 0.\nHundreds:2+5=7 →7, carry 0.\nThousands:9+1=10 →0, carry 1.\nTen thousands:9+3=12 +1=13 →3, carry1.\nHundred thousands:0+9=9 +1=10 →0, carry1.\nMillions:7+5=12 +1=13 →3, carry1.\nTen millions:6+2=8 +1=9 →9, carry0.\nHundred millions:9+1=10 →0, carry1.\n\nNow, since we have a carryover at the hundred millions place, we add a new digit at the beginning: 1. So putting it all together:.\n\n1 (from the carryover) followed by 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 7 (hundreds),9 (tens),0 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 40%|####      | 202/500 [10:26<13:46,  2.77s/it]
