ion 2 (tens): 2.\nPosition 3 (hundreds):5.\nPosition 4 (thousands):2.\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands):7.\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands):2.\nPosition 7 (millions):0.\nPosition 8 (ten-millions):5.\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions):3.\n\nSecond number:471,192,992.\nDigits:.\nPosition 1 (ones):2.\nPosition 2 (tens):9.\nPosition 3 (hundreds):9.\nPosition 4 (thousands):2.\nPosition 5 (ten-thousands):9.\nPosition 6 (hundred-thousands):1.\nPosition 7 (millions):1.\nPosition 8 (ten-millions):7.\nPosition 9 (hundred-millions):4.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let's confirm:.\n\nFor the first number, 350,272,526:.\n\nBreakdown: 350 (million part), 272 (thousand part), 526 (units). So in digits:.\n\nHundred millions:3.\nTen millions:5.\nMillions:0.\nHundred thousands:2.\nTen thousands:7.\nThousands:2.\nHundreds:5.\nTens:2.\nOnes:6.\n\nSimilarly, 471,192,992:.\n\nHundred millions:4.\nTen millions:7.\nMillions:1.\nHundred thousands:1.\nTen thousands:9.\nThousands:2.\nHundreds:9.\nTens:9.\nOnes:2.\n\nSo aligning each position:.\n\nHundred millions:3 vs 4.\nTen millions:5 vs7.\nMillions:0 vs1.\nHundred thousands:2 vs1.\nTen thousands:7 vs9.\nThousands:2 vs2.\nHundreds:5 vs9.\nTens:2 vs9.\nOnes:6 vs2.\n\nNow adding each column starting from ones:.\n\nOnes:6+2=8. So ones digit is 8..\n\nTens:2+9=11. So write 1, carryover 1..\n\nHundreds:5+9=14, plus 1 carried over is 15. Write 5, carryover 1..\n\nThousands:2+2=4, plus 1 carried over=5. No carryover..\n\nTen thousands:7+9=16. Write 6, carryover 1..\n\nHundred thousands:2+1=3, plus 1 carried over=4. No carryover..\n\nMillions:0+1=1. No carryover..\n\nTen millions:5+7=12. Write 2, carryover 1..\n\nHundred millions:3+4=7, plus 1 carried over=8. .\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum would be:.\n\nFrom hundred millions to ones:.\n\n8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),5 (hundreds),1 (tens),8 (ones)..\n\nWait, wait, let me check again:.\n\nWait, after adding:.\n\nHundred millions:3+4=7, plus carryover 1 from ten millions place? Wait, no. Wait, when we added ten millions place (5+7=12), which gives 2 in ten millions and carryover 1 to hundred millions. So 3 (hundred millions of first number) +4 (hundred millions of second) +1 (carryover) =8. So hundred millions digit is 8..\n\nSo the digits are:.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nTen millions:2 (since 5+7=12, write 2, carryover 1).\n\nMillions:0+1=1.\n\nHundred thousands:2+1=3 plus carryover from ten thousands? Wait, ten thousands place was 7+9=16, which is 6 in ten thousands, carryover 1. Then hundred thousands: 2 (from first number) +1 (from second number) =3, plus 1 carried over from ten thousands place: total 4. So hundred thousands is 4..\n\nTen thousands:6..\n\nThousands:5..\n\nHundreds:5..\n\nTens:1..\n\nOnes:8..\n\nWait, so the sum is 8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),5 (hundreds),1 (tens),8 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 42%|####2     | 211/500 [10:56<16:35,  3.44s/it]
