messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 483131226 and 914551499?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out the sum of 483,131,226 and 914,551,499. Hmm, adding two big numbers. Let me start by writing them down one under the other, aligning the digits properly..\n\nSo:.\n\n  483,131,226.\n+914,551,499.\n--------------.\n  .\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's add those first. The ones digits are 6 and 9. 6 + 9 equals 15. Oh, so I write down 5 in the ones place and carry over 1 to the tens place..\n\nNext is the tens place. The digits here are 2 and 9. Adding them gives 2 + 9 = 11, plus the carried-over 1 makes 12. So I put 2 in the tens place and carry over 1 again..\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 2 and 4. 2 + 4 = 6, plus the carryover 1 is 7. So that's 7 in the hundreds place with no carryover now..\n\nThousands place: 1 and 1. 1 + 1 = 2. No carryover here. So 2 in the thousands place..\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 and 5. 3 + 5 = 8. That goes in the ten-thousands spot..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 1 and 4. 1 + 4 = 5. So 5 there..\n\nMillions place: 8 and 1. Wait, wait. Wait, the original numbers are 483,131,226 and 914,551,499. Let me check each digit again to make sure I'm not mixing up places..\n\nWait, maybe I should break down each digit step by step carefully. Let's write both numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 4 8 3 1 3 1 2 2 6.\n\nSecond number:9 1 4 5 5 1 4 9 9.\n\nLet me index them from right to left (ones place to higher places):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6 and 9.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2 and 9.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 2 and 4.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 1 and 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3 and 5.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 1 and 4.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 8 and 1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 4 and 9.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 0 (since the first number has 9 digits, the second number has 9 digits as well, so maybe no leading zeros needed. Wait, 483,131,226 is 9 digits: 4 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 3 (millions), etc. The second number is 914,551,499: 9 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), etc..\n\nSo the digits for each position:.\n\nHundred millions place: 4 and 9.\n\nTen millions place: 8 and 1.\n\nMillions place: 3 and 4.\n\nHundred thousands:1 and5.\n\nTen thousands:3 and5.\n\nThousands:1 and1.\n\nHundreds:2 and4.\n\nTens:2 and9.\n\nOnes:6 and9.\n\nWait, maybe I mixed up some positions earlier. Let me list each digit position correctly:.\n\nFirst number (483,131,226):.\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):6.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):2.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):2.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):3.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):8.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSecond number (914,551,499):.\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1:9.\n\nPosition 2:9.\n\nPosition 3:4.\n\nPosition 4:1.\n\nPosition 5:5.\n\nPosition 6:5.\n\nPosition 7:4.\n\nPosition 8:1.\n\nPosition 9:9.\n\nWait, that might not be correct. Let me parse the second number correctly. 914,551,499. Breaking it down:.\n\nFrom right to left (units first):.\n\nDigits: 9 (ones), 9 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 5 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (hundred millions)..\n\nSo the positions:.\n\n1:9.\n\n2:9.\n\n3:4.\n\n4:1.\n\n5:5.\n\n6:5.\n\n7:4.\n\n8:1.\n\n9:9.\n\nWait, let's confirm:.\n\n914,551,499 is 9 hundred millions, 1 ten million, 4 millions, 5 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 4 hundreds, 9 tens, 9 ones. So yes, that's correct..\n\nFirst number: 483,131,226.\n\nBreakdown: 4 hundred millions, 8 ten millions, 3 millions, 1 hundred thousands, 3 ten thousands, 1 thousand, 2 hundreds, 2 tens, 6 ones..\n\nSo digits from right to left:.\n\nPosition 1:6.\n\nPosition 2:2.\n\nPosition3:2.\n\nPosition4:1.\n\nPosition5:3.\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition8:8.\n\nPosition9:4.\n\nSo when adding, each corresponding position:.\n\nPosition1:6+9=15 (carryover 1).\n\nPosition2:2+9=11 +1=12 (carryover 1).\n\nPosition3:2+4=6 +1=7 (carryover 0).\n\nPosition4:1+1=2 (no carryover).\n\nPosition5:3+5=8 (no carryover).\n\nPosition6:1+5=6 (no carryover).\n\nWait, wait, position6: first number is 1 (hundred thousands), second number is 5 (hundred thousands). 1+5=6. So that's correct..\n\nPosition7:3 (millions) +4 (millions) =7. No carryover..\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions) +1 (ten millions) =9. No carryover..\n\nPosition9:4 (hundred millions) +9 (hundred millions) =13. So here, 3 and carryover 1..\n\nBut since these are the highest digits, the carryover 1 would create a new digit at the billion's place. So total sum would be 1 (billion), 3 (hundred million), etc..\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from position1:.\n\nSum digits:.\n\nPos1: 5, carryover 1.\n\nPos2: 2, carryover1.\n\nPos3:7, carryover0.\n\nPos4:2, carryover0.\n\nPos5:8, carryover0.\n\nPos6:6, carryover0.\n\nPos7:7, carryover0.\n\nPos8:9, carryover0.\n\nPos9:13 (so 3, carryover1).\n\nThen add the carryover 1 as a new digit at position10 (billion place), which is 1..\n\nSo the total sum is 1,3 followed by the rest:.\n\nFrom position9 down: 1 (billion), 3 (hundred million), then positions8-1:.\n\nWait, the digits from highest to lowest (after adding) would be:.\n\nBillion:1.\n\nHundred million:3 +9 (from first number) +9 (second number's hundred millions)? Wait, no. Wait, when we added position9 (hundred millions digits:4 +9=13), so 3 in hundred millions place, carryover1 to billion's place..\n\nThen the rest digits (positions8 to1):.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8+1=9.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3+4=7.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1+5=6.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+5=8.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1+1=2.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+4=6? Wait, no, position3 is hundreds place. Wait, let's recheck:.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a confusion in the positions. Let me list each position properly:.\n\nAfter adding each digit:.\n\nPositions from right (ones) to left:.\n\n1:5.\n\n2:2 (carryover from pos1).\n\nWait, no, when adding, each position is added with the carryover from the previous (right) position..\n\nLet me redo the addition step by step properly:.\n\nStart with carryover =0.\n\nPosition1 (ones):6 +9 +0=15. Write 5, carryover=1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):2 +9 +1=12. Write 2, carryover=1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2 +4 +1=7. Write7, carryover=0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1 +1 +0=2. Write2, carryover=0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 +5 +0=8. Write8, carryover=0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 +5 +0=6. Write6, carryover=0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3 +4 +0=7. Write7, carryover=0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8 +1 +0=9. Write9, carryover=0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4 +9 +0=13. Write3, carryover=1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of1 after the highest digit (hundred millions), we add a new digit (billion's place) with 1..\n\nSo the sum is:.\n\n1 (billion), 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions),7 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),7 (hundreds),2 (tens),5 (ones)..\n\nWait, let me arrange them properly:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit to the lowest:.\n\n1 (billions), then hundred millions (3), ten millions (9), millions (7), hundred thousands (6), ten thousands (8), thousands (2), hundreds (7), tens (2), ones (5)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 45%|####4     | 224/500 [11:33<15:26,  3.36s/it] 45%|####5     | 225/500 [11:36<14:18,  3.12s/it]
