rom first number) +8 (from second) +1 (carry)=10, so 0 and carry 1. Ten-millions: 2 +5 +1=8. Then hundred-millions:3+2=5. So yes, the digits are 5,8,0,4,4,0,0,0,2. So the sum is 580,440,002..\n\nWait, let me verify using another method. Maybe subtract one of the numbers from the total to see if I get the other. Let's take 580,440,002 minus 321,841,309. That should equal 285,598,693..\n\n580,440,002 minus 321,841,309. Let's subtract:.\n\nStarting from the ones: 2 -9. Can't do, borrow. 2 becomes 12, subtract 9 is 3. Next digit (tens): 0 becomes 9 (after borrowing), subtract 0 is 9. Hundreds: 0 becomes 9 (borrowed), subtract 3? Wait, no, the second number is 321,841,309. So the digits are 3 2 1 8 4 1 3 0 9..\n\nWait, the subtraction would be:.\n\n580,440,002.\n-321,841,309.\n= ?.\n\nLet me line them up:.\n\n5 8 0 4 4 0 0 0 2.\n-3 2 1 8 4 1 3 0 9.\n---------------------.\nStarting from ones: 2-9. Can't do, borrow. So borrow from tens place. Tens is 0, so have to go all the way to hundreds place, which is also 0. Then thousands place, 0. Ten-thousands: 4. So borrow from ten-thousands place. So ten-thousands digit becomes 3, then thousands gets 10. Then hundreds gets 10 from thousands. Then tens get 10 from hundreds. Then ones get 10 from tens. So 12 -9=3. Tens: 9-0=9. Hundreds:9-3=6. Thousands:9-1=8. Ten-thousands:3-4. Wait, the ten-thousands digit in the first number after borrowing is 3, subtract the second number's ten-thousands digit which is 8? Wait, no, the second number's digits are 3 (hundred-millions), 2 (ten-millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 9 (ones). So when subtracting:.\n\nHundred-millions:5-3=2.\nTen-millions:8-2=6.\nMillions:0-1. Can't do, borrow from hundred-millions. So 8 becomes 7, and 0 becomes 10. Then 10-1=9. Wait, this is getting complicated. Maybe I made a mistake here. Alternatively, perhaps my initial addition was wrong..\n\nAlternatively, let me use another check. Let's add 