t (ones place):.\n\n6 (ones).\n5 (tens).\n4 (hundreds).\n2 (thousands).\n6 (ten thousands).\n3 (hundred thousands).\n1 (millions).\n4 (ten millions).\n8 (hundred millions).\n\nNumber2: 199,915,371.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n1 (ones).\n7 (tens).\n3 (hundreds).\n5 (thousands).\n1 (ten thousands).\n9 (hundred thousands).\n9 (millions).\n9 (ten millions).\n1 (hundred millions?) Wait, wait. Wait, 199,915,371 is 1 followed by 99,915,371. Let me count the digits:.\n\n1 (hundred millions place?), then 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands?), wait maybe I should write each digit properly..\n\nWait, maybe it's better to write each number with their digits separated:.\n\nNumber1: 8 (hundred millions) 4 (ten millions) 1 (millions) 3 (hundred thousands) 6 (ten thousands) 2 (thousands) 4 (hundreds) 5 (tens) 6 (ones).\n\nNumber2: 1 (hundred millions) 9 (ten millions) 9 (millions) 9 (hundred thousands) 1 (ten thousands) 5 (thousands) 3 (hundreds) 7 (tens) 1 (ones).\n\nWait, no. Wait, 199,915,371 is 1 followed by 99, then 915,371. Let's parse it correctly..\n\nLet me write out each number digit by digit from the largest place to the smallest..\n\nNumber1: 841,362,456.\n\nDigits (from left to right):.\n- Hundred millions: 8.\n- Ten millions:4.\n- Millions:1.\n- Hundred thousands:3.\n- Ten thousands:6.\n- Thousands:2.\n- Hundreds:4.\n- Tens:5.\n- Ones:6.\n\nNumber2:199,915,371.\n\nDigits:.\n- Hundred millions:1 (since 199 million is part of it).\n- Ten millions:9.\n- Millions:9.\n- Hundred thousands:9.\n- Ten thousands:1.\n- Thousands:5.\n- Hundreds:3.\n- Tens:7.\n- Ones:1.\n\nWait, yes. Because 199,915,371 is 1*100,000,000 + 9*10,000,000 +9*1,000,000 +9*100,000 +1*10,000 +5*1,000 +3*100 +7*10 +1*1..\n\nSo aligning both numbers:.\n\nHundred millions: 8 vs 1.\nTen millions:4 vs9.\nMillions:1 vs9.\nHundred thousands:3 vs9.\nTen thousands:6 vs1.\nThousands:2 vs5.\nHundreds:4 vs3.\nTens:5 vs7.\nOnes:6 vs1.\n\nNow, adding each column starting from the ones:.\n\nOnes place: 6 +1 =7. So ones digit is 7, no carryover..\n\nTens place:5 +7=12. Write 2, carryover 1..\n\nHundreds:4 +3=7, plus carryover 1 makes 8. No carryover..\n\nThousands:2 +5=7. No carryover..\n\nTen thousands:6 +1=7. No carryover..\n\nHundred thousands:3 +9=12. Write 2, carryover 1..\n\nMillions place:1 +9=10, plus carryover 1=11. Write 1, carryover 1..\n\nTen millions:4 +9=13, plus carryover 1=14. Write 4, carryover 1..\n\nHundred millions:8 +1=9, plus carryover 1=10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n\nBut wait, since we're at the hundred millions place, adding 8 (from number1) +1 (from number2) =9, plus the carryover 1 gives 10. So we need to create a new digit to the left. So the hundred millions place becomes 0, and carryover 1 to the billions place, which is 1..\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nFrom right to left (after addition):.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nTens:2.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nHundred thousands:2 (with carryover 1).\n\nMillions:1 (with carryover 1).\n\nTen millions:4 (with carryover 1).\n\nHundred millions:0 (with carryover 1).\n\nThen the billions place:1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 48%|####8     | 240/500 [12:23<12:41,  2.93s/it] 48%|####8     | 241/500 [12:26<12:38,  2.93s/it]
