e write down 1 and carry over 1 to the next higher digit..\n\nNow the hundred thousands place: 8 (from first number) and 6 (from second number). Adding those gives 8 + 6 = 14. But we have a carry-over of 1 from the previous step, so 14 + 1 = 15. So we write down 5 and carry over 1 again..\n\nMoving to the millions place: 9 (from first number) and 1 (from second number). 9 + 1 = 10, plus the carried 1 makes 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1..\n\nNext, the ten millions place: 6 (from first number) and 1 (from second number). 6 + 1 = 7, plus the carried 1 makes 8. No carry here..\n\nThen the hundred millions place: 4 (from first number) and 4 (from second number). 4 + 4 = 8. No carry-over..\n\nWait, but the first number is 469 million, which breaks down as 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 9 (millions). The second number is 411 million, which is 4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions). So after adding up to the hundred millions place, we have 4 + 4 = 8. So all digits are accounted for</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
