ndred millions).\n- 4 (ten millions).\n- 8 (millions).\n- 5 (hundred thousands).\n- 2 (ten thousands).\n- 1 (thousands).\n- 2 (hundreds).\n- 9 (tens).\n- 9 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 937,024,529:.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions).\n- 3 (ten millions).\n- 7 (millions).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 2 (ten thousands).\n- 4 (thousands).\n- 5 (hundreds).\n- 2 (tens).\n- 9 (ones).\n\nWait, so when I was adding the millions place earlier, the first number had 8 (since 748 million is 7 in hundred millions, 4 in ten millions, 8 in millions). The second number has 7 in millions. So 8 + 7 = 15. Wait, wait, that's where I might have messed up earlier. Let me redo that part..\n\nSo, let's go step by step again, properly aligning each digit:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones: 9 + 9 = 18 → write 8, carryover 1..\n2. Tens: 9 + 2 = 11 + 1 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1..\n3. Hundreds: 2 + 5 = 7 + 1 = 8 → write 8, no carryover..\n4. Thousands: 1 + 4 = 5 → write 5..\n5. Ten thousands: 2 + 0 = 2 → write 2..\n6. Hundred thousands: 5 + 2 = 7 → write 7..\n7. Millions: 8 + 7 = 15 → write 5, carryover 1..\n8. Ten millions: 4 + 3 = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8 → write 8..\n9. Hundred millions: 7 + 9 = 16 → write 6, carryover 1..\n10. Then, since there's a carryover 1 left, we need to add a new digit to the left: 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
