 millions): 6.\n\nSecond number: 968,807,354.\n\nDigits (from right to left):.\n\nPosition 1: 4.\nPosition 2: 5.\nPosition 3: 3.\nPosition 4: 7.\nPosition 5: 0.\nPosition 6: 8.\nPosition 7: 8.\nPosition 8: 6.\nPosition 9: 9.\n\nWait, no. Wait, the second number is 968,807,354. Let's break it down:.\n\nDigits from right (ones) to left:.\n\nPosition 1: 4 (ones).\nPosition 2: 5 (tens).\nPosition 3: 3 (hundreds).\nPosition 4: 7 (thousands).\nPosition 5: 0 (ten thousands).\nPosition 6: 8 (hundred thousands).\nPosition 7: 8 (millions).\nPosition 8: 6 (ten millions).\nPosition 9: 9 (hundred millions).\n\nOkay, now adding each position from ones to the highest:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4 + 4 = 8. No carry-over..\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 1 + 5 = 6. Sum is 6. No carry-over..\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 6 + 3 = 9. Sum 9. No carry-over..\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 2 + 7 = 9. Sum 9. No carry-over..\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 9 + 0 = 9. Sum 9..\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 2 (from first number) + 8 (from second) = 10. Oh, here's a carry-over. So sum is 0 here, carry over 1 to the next position..\n\nPosition 7 (millions): First number has 0, second has 8. So 0 + 8 = 8, plus the carried-over 1 makes 9. So sum here is 9, carry-over 0..\n\nWait, wait. Let me check again. Wait, the first number is 650,292,614. Breaking it down:.\n\nDigits (from left to right):.\n\n6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 4 (ones)..\n\nWait, no. Wait, 650,292,614: Let's count the digits:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 4.\n\nTens:1.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nSimilarly for the second number 968,807,354:.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nTens:5.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nHundred millions:9.\n\nSo when adding, position 7 is millions. For the first number, millions place is 0. Second number's millions place is 8. Then adding 0 +8=8. But earlier at position 6 (hundred thousands), we had a carry-over of 1. Wait, position 6 (hundred thousands) sum was 2 +8=10. So sum is 0, carry-over 1 to position 7 (millions). So position 7: 0 +8 +1=9. Then no carry-over here..\n\nMoving to position 8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 5, second number's is 6. 5 +6=11. Plus any carry-over? From position 7, there was no carry-over. So sum is 11. So we write 1 and carry over 1 to the next position..\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 6, second is 9. 6 +9=15, plus the carried-over 1 makes 16. So sum is 6, carry-over 1. But since there are no higher digits, we write 16 as 6 and carry-over 1, but that would add a new digit at the beginning. So the total sum would be 1 (from the carry) followed by 6, then the rest..\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nTens:6.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nHundred thousands:0 (with carry-over 1).\n\nMillions:9.\n\nTen millions:1 (since 5+6=11, write 1 carry 1).\n\nHundred millions:6 +9 +1 (carry) =16, so 6 with carry 1..\n\nThen, since we have a carry-over of 1 at the hundred millions place, which is the highest digit here, we add that as a new digit at the beginning: 1. So the total digits are:.\n\n1 (billions place), 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones)..\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the sum step by step:.\n\nAfter adding each digit with carries:.\n\nPosition 1:8.\n\nPosition2:6.\n\nPosition3:9.\n\nPosition4:9.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:0 (carry 1).\n\nPosition7:9 (0+8+1=9).\n\nPosition8:1 (5+6=11, write 1 carry 1).\n\nPosition9:6 (6+9=15 +1=16, write 6 carry 1).\n\nThen, the carry-over from position9:1, so add a new position at the beginning (billions):1.\n\nSo the sum is 1,6 (from position9),1 (position8),9 (position7),0 (position6),9 (position5),9 (position4),9 (position3),6 (position2),8 (position1)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 50%|####9     | 249/500 [12:46<10:50,  2.59s/it] 50%|#####     | 250/500 [12:49<10:49,  2.60s/it]
