messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 436064957 and 631579777?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 436,064,957 and 631,579,777. Hmm, adding two large numbers. I should start by writing them down one under the other, aligning the digits properly by place value. Let me set it up:.\n\n  436,064,957.\n+ 631,579,777.\n----------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's add each column step by step and keep track of any carry-over..\n\nOnes place: 7 + 7 = 14. So I write down 4 and carry over 1..\n\nTens place: 5 + 7 = 12, plus the carried 1 makes 13. Write 3, carry 1..\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 7 = 16, plus 1 is 17. Write 7, carry 1..\n\nThousands place: 4 + 9 = 13, plus 1 is 14. Write 4, carry 1..\n\nTen-thousands place: 6 + 7 = 13, plus 1 is 14. Write 4, carry 1..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 0 + 5 = 5, plus 1 is 6. No carry here..\n\nMillions place: 6 + 1 = 7. Wait, wait. Wait, the first number is 436,064,957. Let me check each digit again to make sure I'm not mixing up positions..\n\nWait, breaking down both numbers:.\n\n436,064,957:.\n- 4 (hundred millions).\n- 3 (ten millions).\n- 6 (millions).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 4 (thousands).\n- 9 (hundreds).\n- 5 (tens).\n- 7 (ones).\n\n631,579,777:.\n- 6 (hundred millions).\n- 3 (ten millions).\n- 1 (millions).\n- 5 (hundred thousands).\n- 7 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 7 (hundreds).\n- 7 (tens).\n- 7 (ones).\n\nWait, so when adding the millions place: 6 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 7. Then, carry over if any. But earlier steps: let me recount..\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the previous steps. Let's redo the addition carefully, digit by digit..\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones: 7 + 7 = 14 → write 4, carry 1..\n\n2. Tens: 5 + 7 = 12 + 1 = 13 → write 3, carry 1..\n\n3. Hundreds: 9 + 7 = 16 +1 =17 → write 7, carry 1..\n\n4. Thousands: 4 +9 =13 +1=14 → write 4, carry 1..\n\n5. Ten thousands:6 +7=13 +1=14 → write 4, carry 1..\n\n6. Hundred thousands:0 +5=5 +1=6 → write 6, no carry..\n\n7. Millions:6 +1=7 → write 7, no carry..\n\n8. Ten millions:3 +3=6 → write 6, no carry..\n\n9. Hundred millions:4 +6=10 → write 0, carry 1..\n\nBut wait, the next place after hundred millions is billions. Since both numbers are 9-digit, adding them could result in a 10-digit number. So after adding hundred millions place, we have a carryover of 1. So we need to add that as a new digit at the beginning..\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from ones:.\n\nDigits (from right to left):.\n\n1. 4 (carry 1).\n\n2. 3 (carry 1).\n\n3.7 (carry1).\n\n4.4 (carry1).\n\n5.4 (carry1).\n\n6.6 (no carry).\n\n7.7 (no carry).\n\n8.6 (no carry).\n\n9.0 (carry1).\n\nThen the final carryover 1 becomes the next digit..\n\nSo the sum would be 1,0 (billion), then 6 (ten millions?), wait maybe I need to line it up properly..\n\nWait, let's write each digit position with their results:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones: 4.\n\n2. Tens:3.\n\n3. Hundreds:7.\n\n4. Thousands:4.\n\n5. Ten thousands:4.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:6.\n\n7. Millions:7.\n\n8. Ten millions:6.\n\n9. Hundred millions:0.\n\nPlus the carried over 1 billion</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
