llion), 6 (million), 8 (hundred thousand), 6 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 0 (ten), 6 (one)..\n\nWait, so in the ten-millions place, the first number is 2, and the second is 4. So 2 + 4 = 6. Plus any carryover? Let me check previous steps. Wait, the hundred-thousands place was 0 + 8 +1 =9, so no carryover there. Then in the millions place, the first number's million digit is 3, the second's is 6. So 3 +6 =9. Wait, but earlier step said million place: first is 2, second is 6. Wait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let me write the numbers aligned properly:.\n\n123,074,397.\n546,869,806.\n\nAdding them:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 7 +6 =13 → 3, carry 1..\n\nTens:9 +0 =9 +1=10 →0, carry 1..\n\nHundreds:3 +8=11 +1=12 →2, carry1..\n\nThousands:4 +6=10 +1=11 →1, carry1..\n\nTen-thousands:7 +9=16 +1=17 →7, carry1..\n\nHundred-thousands:0 +8=8 +1=9 →9, carry0..\n\nMillions:3 +6=9 →9, no carry..\n\nTen-millions:2 +4=6 →6..\n\nHundred-millions:1 +5=6..\n\nPutting it all together: 6 (hundred million), 6 (ten million),9 (million),9 (hundred thousand),7 (ten thousand),1 (thousand),2 (hundred),0 (ten),3 (one)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
