s):9 and 0.\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3 and 7.\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):9 and 6.\nPosition 7 (millions):2 and 0.\nPosition 8 (ten millions):8 and 8.\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):8 and 1.\n\nNow adding each position:.\n\n1. Ones: 0 + 0 = 0.\n2. Tens:4 + 0 =4.\n3. Hundreds:5 +0=5.\n4. Thousands:9 +0=9.\n5. Ten thousands:3 +7=10. So write 0, carryover 1..\n6. Hundred thousands:9 +6 =15, plus carryover 1 =16. Write 6, carryover 1..\n7. Millions:2 +0=2 +1 (carryover) =3..\n8. Ten millions:8 +8=16. Write 6, carryover 1..\n9. Hundred millions:8 +1=9 +1 (carryover)=10. So write 0, carryover 1. Since there are no higher places, that carryover becomes a new digit at the front..\n\nPutting it all together from right to left:.\n\nStart with the digits we calculated:.\n\nPosition1:0.\nPosition2:4.\nPosition3:5.\nPosition4:9.\nPosition5:0 (with carryover 1).\nPosition6:6 (carryover 1).\nPosition7:3.\nPosition8:6 (carryover 1).\nPosition9:0 (carryover 1 from position9 addition).\nThen the final carryover 1 from position9 becomes the next digit..\n\nSo assembling the digits from highest to lowest (left to right):.\n\nThe final digits would be:.\n\n1 (from the last carryover) followed by 0 (from position9), then 6 (position8), 3 (position7), 6 (position6), 0 (position5), 9 (position4),5 (position3),4 (position2),0 (position1)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
