2: 3 (tens).\n3: 9 (hundreds).\n4: 1 (thousands).\n5: 6 (ten thousands).\n6: 1 (hundred thousands).\n7: 8 (millions).\n8: 1 (ten millions).\n9: 8 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, maybe I should list them aligned:.\n\nFirst number: 7 6 5 7 5 6 1 2 5.\n\nSecond number:8 1 8 1 6 1 9 3 3.\n\nSo when adding, starting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 5 + 3 = 8.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2 + 3 = 5.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 1 + 9 = 10. Write 0, carry 1..\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 6 (from first) + 1 (from second) + carryover 1 = 8. So 6 +1=7, plus 1 is 8. No carryover here..\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):5 (first) +6 (second) =11. Write 1, carryover 1..\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):7 (first) +1 (second) =8, plus carryover 1 makes 9. No carryover..\n\nPosition 7 (millions):5 (first) +8 (second) =13. Write 3, carryover 1..\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):6 (first) +1 (second) =7, plus carryover 1 makes 8. No carryover..\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):7 (first) +8 (second) =15. Write 5, carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the left, making the sum start with 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
