messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 443365463 and 428160067?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's tackle adding 443,365,463 and 428,160,067. Hmm, adding two big numbers. Let me write them down aligned properly to make sure each digit is in the right place..\n\nFirst, let's break down each number by their digits:.\n\n443,365,463.\n428,160,067.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nOnes place: 3 + 7 = 10. Oh, so that's 0 with a carryover of 1..\n\nTens place: 6 + 6 = 12, plus the carried over 1 makes 13. So that's 3 here, carryover 1..\n\nHundreds place: 4 + 0 = 4, plus 1 is 5. No carryover here..\n\nThousands place: 5 + 0 = 5. That's straightforward..\n\nTen-thousands place: 6 + 6 = 12. Write down 2, carryover 1..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 1 = 4, plus the 1 carried over is 5..\n\nMillions place: 4 + 8 = 12. So 2 here, carryover 1..\n\nTen-millions place: 4 + 2 = 6, plus the 1 is 7..\n\nHundred-millions place: 4 + 4 = 8. Wait, no, wait. Wait, the first number is 443 million, so the digits are 4 (hundred million), 4 (ten million), 3 (million). But the second number is 428 million, which is 4 (hundred million), 2 (ten million), 8 (million). Wait, maybe I messed up the places here..\n\nWait, let me check again. Let's write both numbers properly:.\n\n443,365,463.\n\nvs.\n\n428,160,067.\n\nBreaking them into digits:.\n\nFirst number (443,365,463):.\n\n- 4 (hundred millions).\n- 4 (ten millions).\n- 3 (millions).\n- 3 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 5 (thousands).\n- 4 (hundreds).\n- 6 (tens).\n- 3 (ones).\n\nSecond number (428,160,067):.\n\n- 4 (hundred millions).\n- 2 (ten millions).\n- 8 (millions).\n- 1 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 0 (thousands).\n- 0 (hundreds).\n- 6 (tens).\n- 7 (ones).\n\nSo when adding each corresponding place:.\n\nStarting from ones:.\n\nOnes: 3 + 7 = 10 → 0, carryover 1..\n\nTens: 6 + 6 = 12 + 1 = 13 → 3, carryover 1..\n\nHundreds: 4 + 0 = 4 + 1 = 5 → 5, no carryover..\n\nThousands: 5 + 0 = 5 → 5..\n\nTen thousands: 6 + 6 = 12 → 2, carryover 1..\n\nHundred thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5 → 5..\n\nMillions: 3 + 8 = 11 → 1, carryover 1..\n\nTen millions: 4 + 2 = 6 + 1 = 7 → 7..\n\nHundred millions: 4 + 4 = 8 → 8..\n\nWait, but when adding the millions place: first number has 3 (millions?), wait, the first number is 443 million. Wait, 443,365,463: the digits are 4 (hundred million), 4 (ten million), 3 (million). Then the next digits are 3 (hundred thousand), etc. So when adding millions place, first number has 3, second has 8. So 3 +8 =11. So write down 1, carryover 1. Then ten millions place: 4 (from first) + 2 (from second) =6, plus carryover 1 makes 7. Hundred millions: 4 +4 =8..\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 0.\n\nTens:3.\n\nHundreds:5.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nMillions:1 (with carryover 1).\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nWait, but the hundred thousands place was 3 (from first number) +1 (from second number) plus carryover 1 from ten thousands place?.\n\nWait, let me recheck each step again step by step to avoid mistakes..\n\nLet me write both numbers vertically:.\n\n  443,365,463.\n\n+ 428,160,067.\n\n----------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\n1. Ones place: 3 +7 =10 → write 0, carryover 1..\n\n2. Tens place:6 +6=12, plus 1 =13 → write 3, carryover 1..\n\n3. Hundreds place:4 +0=4, plus 1 =5 → write 5, no carryover..\n\n4. Thousands place:5 +0=5 → write 5..\n\n5. Ten thousands place:6 +6=12 → write 2, carryover 1..\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:3 +1=4, plus 1=5 → write 5..\n\n7. Millions place:3 +8=11 → write 1, carryover 1..\n\n8. Ten millions place:4 +2=6, plus 1=7 → write7..\n\n9. Hundred millions place:4 +4=8 → write8..\n\nSo combining all the digits from the hundred millions to ones: 8 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),1 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),5 (hundreds),3 (tens),0 (ones). Wait, wait, that can't be right. Wait, let's see:.\n\nWait, after millions place, the next digits are hundred thousands, ten thousands, etc. Wait, maybe I missed some places. Let me check:.\n\nWait, the places are:.\n\n- Hundred millions (9th digit from the right).\n\n- Ten millions (8th).\n\n- Millions (7th).\n\n- Hundred thousands (6th).\n\n- Ten thousands (5th).\n\n- Thousands (4th).\n\n- Hundreds (3rd).\n\n- Tens (2nd).\n\n- Ones (1st).\n\nWait, perhaps the order is:.\n\nWhen writing the sum, the digits would be:.\n\nFrom right to left:.\n\nOnes (1st), Tens (2nd), Hundreds (3rd), Thousands (4th), Ten thousands (5th), Hundred thousands (6th), Millions (7th), Ten millions (8th), Hundred millions (9th)..\n\nSo after adding each position:.\n\nOnes: 0.\n\nTens:3.\n\nHundreds:5.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nSo putting them in order from hundred millions to ones:.\n\n8 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions),1 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),5 (hundreds),3 (tens),0 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 64%|######3   | 319/500 [16:09<08:25,  2.79s/it] 64%|######4   | 320/500 [16:11<08:16,  2.76s/it]
