messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 935131196 and 169164213?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 935,131,196 and 169,164,213. Hmm, adding two large numbers. Let me start by writing them down vertically to line up the digits properly..\n\nSo, 935,131,196.\nplus 169,164,213.\n________________.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's go step by step..\n\nOnes place: 6 + 3 = 9. So the ones digit of the sum is 9. No carry-over here because it's less than 10..\n\nNext, the tens place: 9 + 1 = 10. Oh, wait, that's 10. So I write down 0 in the tens place and carry over 1 to the hundreds place..\n\nHundreds place: 1 (carry) + 1 + 2 = 4. So that's 4 in the hundreds place. No carry-over..\n\nThousands place: 9 + 4 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1..\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 (carry) + 1 + 6 = 8. So 8 there. No carry..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 1 = 4. Wait, wait. Let me check again. The original numbers are 935,131,196 and 169,164,213. Let me break them into digits:.\n\nFirst number: 9 3 5 1 3 1 1 9 6.\n\nSecond number: 1 6 9 1 6 4 2 1 3.\n\nWait, maybe I should write each digit position by position to avoid confusion. Let me list them properly:.\n\nPositions (from right, ones to billions):.\n\nFirst number (935,131,196):.\n\n- Ones: 6.\n- Tens: 9.\n- Hundreds: 1.\n- Thousands: 1.\n- Ten thousands: 3.\n- Hundred thousands: 1.\n- Millions: 5.\n- Ten millions: 3.\n- Hundred millions: 9.\n\nSecond number (169,164,213):.\n\n- Ones: 3.\n- Tens: 1.\n- Hundreds: 2.\n- Thousands: 4.\n- Ten thousands: 6.\n- Hundred thousands: 1.\n- Millions: 9.\n- Ten millions: 6.\n- Hundred millions: 1.\n\nNow, adding each position starting from ones:.\n\n1. Ones: 6 + 3 = 9 → sum digit 9, carry 0.\n2. Tens: 9 + 1 = 10 → sum digit 0, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds: 1 + 2 = 3 + carry 1 → 4 → sum digit 4, carry 0.\n4. Thousands: 1 + 4 = 5 → sum 5, carry 0.\nWait, wait. Wait, first number's thousands digit is 1 (from 935,131,196 which is ... 1 in the thousands place?), let me confirm. Let me parse 935,131,196 correctly..\n\nBreaking down 935,131,196:.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions).\n- 3 (ten millions).\n- 5 (millions).\n- 1 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 1 (thousands).\n- 1 (hundreds).\n- 9 (tens).\n- 6 (ones).\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, maybe I messed up the positions. Let me think again. The number is 935,131,196..\n\nBreaking down each group:.\n\n- Millions group: 935 (but wait, 935 million?), wait, no. Wait, numbers are separated as 935,131,196. So that's 935 million, 131 thousand, 196. So:.\n\n- Hundred millions: 9.\n- Ten millions: 3.\n- Millions: 5.\n- Hundred thousands: 1.\n- Ten thousands: 3.\n- Thousands: 1.\n- Hundreds: 1.\n- Tens: 9.\n- Ones: 6.\n\nSimilarly, 169,164,213 is 169 million, 164 thousand, 213. So:.\n\n- Hundred millions: 1.\n- Ten millions: 6.\n- Millions: 9.\n- Hundred thousands: 1.\n- Ten thousands: 6.\n- Thousands: 4.\n- Hundreds: 2.\n- Tens: 1.\n- Ones: 3.\n\nSo now, let's list each position:.\n\nPosition (from right to left, ones first):.\n\n1. Ones: 6 (first) + 3 (second) = 9 → sum 9, carry 0.\n2. Tens: 9 + 1 = 10 → sum 0, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds: 1 + 2 = 3 + carry 1 = 4 → sum 4, carry 0.\n4. Thousands: 1 (first number) + 4 (second) = 5 → sum 5, carry 0.\n5. Ten thousands: 3 (first) + 6 (second) = 9 → sum 9, carry 0.\n6. Hundred thousands: 1 (first) + 1 (second) = 2 → sum 2, carry 0.\n7. Millions: 5 (first) + 9 (second) = 14 → sum 4, carry 1.\n8. Ten millions: 3 (first) + 6 (second) = 9 + carry 1 = 10 → sum 0, carry 1.\n9. Hundred millions: 9 (first) + 1 (second) = 10 + carry 1 = 11 → sum 1, carry 1 (but since we're at the hundred millions place, the next is billions place).\n\nWait, after hundred millions, the next is billions. So adding the hundred millions digits:.\n\n9 (first number) + 1 (second number) = 10, plus carry 1 from the ten millions place makes 11. So we write 1 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the billions place..\n\nBut since there are no higher digits, the carryover 1 becomes the billions digit..\n\nPutting it all together from right to left (ones to billions):.\n\nSum digits:.\n\n- Ones:9.\n- Tens:0.\n- Hundreds:4.\n- Thousands:5.\n- Ten thousands:9.\n- Hundred thousands:2.\n- Millions:4.\n- Ten millions:0 (since 10 in ten millions place, wrote 0 and carried 1).\n- Hundred millions:1 (from 11, wrote 1 and carried 1).\n- Billions:1 (the carryover).\n\nSo the sum would be 1 (billion), 1 (hundred million), 0 (ten million), 4 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 5 (thousand), 4 (hundred), 0 (ten), 9 (ones). Wait, that can't be right. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the order..\n\nWait, when writing the digits from the highest place to lowest, it should be:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:1 (from 9+1+1 carryover).\n\nTen millions:0 (from 3+6+1=10, so 0 with carryover).\n\nMillions:4 (5+9=14, so 4 with carry 1).\n\nHundred thousands:1 (from first number) +1 (second) + carryover? Wait, no. Wait, let me reconstruct the sum step by step again carefully..\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the millions and ten millions places. Let's redo the addition step by step with each position:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n1. Ones: 6 + 3 = 9 → 9, carry 0.\n2. Tens:9 +1=10 → 0, carry 1.\n3. Hundreds:1 +2=3 +1=4 → 4, carry 0.\n4. Thousands:1 +4=5 →5, carry 0.\n5. Ten thousands:3 +6=9 →9, carry 0.\n6. Hundred thousands:1 +1=2 →2, carry 0.\n7. Millions:5 +9=14 →4, carry 1.\n8. Ten millions:3 +6=9 +1 (carry) =10 →0, carry1.\n9. Hundred millions:9 +1=10 +1 (carry)=11 →1, carry1.\n10. Billions: carry1.\n\nSo combining from billions down:.\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),4 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),4 (hundreds),0 (tens),9 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 64%|######4   | 322/500 [16:17<07:49,  2.64s/it] 65%|######4   | 323/500 [16:19<07:54,  2.68s/it]
