rry1).\nTen-thousands:3+9=12 +1=13 (carry1).\nHundred-thousands:9+5=14 +1=15 (carry1).\nMillions:6+7=13 +1=14 (carry1).\nTen-millions:9+9=18 +1=19 (carry1).\nHundred-millions:9+3=12 +1=13..\n\nWait, but the first number's hundred-millions place is 9 (since it's 996 million), and the second number's hundred-millions is 3 (379 million). So adding those: 9+3=12, plus the carryover 1 from the ten-millions place makes 13. So that would be 13 in the hundred-millions place. But since we're dealing with each digit place, the hundred-millions place can only hold a single digit. So we write 3 here and carry over 1 to the next place, which would be the billions place. Since there are no higher digits in the original numbers, that carryover 1 becomes the next digit</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
