messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 226798201 and 809216897?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's tackle the addition of 226,798,201 and 809,216,897. Hmm, adding these two big numbers. I think I should line them up by place value first. Let me write them down vertically to make it easier:.\n\n    226,798,201.\n  +809,216,897.\n  =============.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The ones digits are 1 and 7. Adding those gives 8. So, the ones place of the sum is 8. No carry-over here because 1 + 7 is 8, which is less than 10..\n\nNext, moving to the tens place. The digits are 0 and 9. Wait, 0 + 9 equals 9. So the tens place is 9. Still no carry-over..\n\nNow the hundreds place: 2 and 8. 2 + 8 is 10. Oh, here we have a carry-over. So the hundreds place is 0, and we carry over 1 to the thousands place..\n\nThousands place: 8 (from the first number) plus 6 (from the second number) plus the carried-over 1. Let's see, 8 + 6 is 14, plus 1 makes 15. So the thousands place is 5, and we carry over 1 again..\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 (from first number) and 1 (from second number) plus the carry-over 1. 9 + 1 is 10, plus 1 is 11. So the ten-thousands digit is 1, carry over 1..\n\nHundred-thousands place: 7 (from first number) and 2 (from second number) plus the 1 carried over. 7 + 2 is 9, plus 1 is 10. So here it's 0 with another carry-over of 1..\n\nMillions place: 6 (first number) and 6 (second number) plus 1. 6 + 6 is 12, plus 1 is 13. So the millions place is 3, carry over 1..\n\nTen-millions place: 2 (first number) and 0 (second number) plus 1. 2 + 0 +1 = 3. So that's 3 here..\n\nHundred-millions place: 2 (first number) and 8 (second number). 2 + 8 = 10. So here we put 0 and carry over 1 to the billions place..\n\nFinally, the billions place: There's only the carried-over 1. So the sum is 1 followed by 0, 3, 3, 0, 1, 5, 9, 8. Wait, let me check that again..\n\nPutting all the digits together from the highest place to the lowest:.\n\nHundred-millions place: 0 (from 2 +8=10, carry 1).\nTen-millions: 3 (2 +0 +1).\nMillions: 3 (6 +6 +1=13).\nHundred-thousands:0 (7+2+1=10).\nTen-thousands:1 (9+1+1=11).\nThousands:5 (8+6+1=15).\nHundreds:0 (2+8=10).\nTens:9 (0+9).\nOnes:8 (1+7).\n\nWait, but when adding the hundred-millions place: 2 (from first number's hundred-millions place) and 8 (from second number's hundred-millions place) gives 10, so we write 0 there and carry 1 to the billions place. Then the ten-millions place: first number has 2, second has 0, plus the carry 1, so 3. Millions place: first number's 6 and second's 6 plus carry 1 is 13, so 3 with carry 1. Then hundred-thousands place: first number's 7, second's 2, plus carry 1: 10, so 0 carry 1. Ten-thousands: 9 +1 (from second number's ten-thousands place?) Wait, wait, let me check the original numbers again..\n\nWait, first number is 226,798,201. Breaking it down:.\n\n- 2 (hundred millions).\n- 2 (ten millions).\n- 6 (millions).\n- 7 (hundred thousands).\n- 9 (ten thousands).\n- 8 (thousands).\n- 2 (hundreds).\n- 0 (tens).\n- 1 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 809,216,897.\n\nBreakdown:.\n\n- 8 (hundred millions).\n- 0 (ten millions).\n- 9 (millions) Wait, wait, no. Wait, 809,216,897: Let's parse this correctly..\n\nWait, 809,216,897. Let's separate the digits:.\n\nStarting from the right: 8 (hundreds place), 9 (tens), 7 (ones) — no, wait, wait. Wait, numbers are written as:.\n\n809,216,897 is:.\n\nHundred millions: 8.\n\nTen millions: 0.\n\nMillions:9.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nTens:9.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nWait, wait, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me count the digits properly..\n\nFirst number: 226,798,201 has digits (from right to left):.\n\n1 (ones), 0 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions)..\n\nSecond number: 809,216,897:.\n\nDigits from right to left: 7 (ones),9 (tens),8 (hundreds),6 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),2 (hundred thousands),9 (millions),0 (ten millions),8 (hundred millions)..\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nHundred millions place: 2 (from first number) +8 (from second) =10. So 0 here, carry 1..\n\nTen millions place:2 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carry) =3. So 3..\n\nMillions place:6 (first) +9 (second). Wait, no: Wait, original numbers: first number's millions place is 6, second's is 9? Wait, the first number is 226,798,201. So the digits are:.\n\n2 (hundred million), 2 (ten million), 6 (million), 7 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 8 (thousand), 2 (hundred), 0 (ten), 1 (one)..\n\nSecond number: 809,216,897:.\n\n8 (hundred million), 0 (ten million), 9 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 1 (ten thousand),6 (thousand),8 (hundred),9 (ten),7 (one)..\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n1 (from first) +7 (second) =8. Ones digit:8..\n\nTens place:0 (first) +9 (second) =9. Tens digit:9..\n\nHundreds place:2 (first) +8 (second)=10. So 0 here, carry 1..\n\nThousands place:8 (first) +6 (second) +1 (carry) =15. So 5 here, carry 1..\n\nTen thousands place:9 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry) =11. So 1 here, carry 1..\n\nHundred thousands place:7 (first) +2 (second) +1 (carry) =10. So 0 here, carry 1..\n\nMillions place:6 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry) =16. So 6 here, carry 1..\n\nTen millions place:2 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carry) =3. So 3 here..\n\nHundred millions place:2 (first) +8 (second) =10. So 0 here, carry 1..\n\nThen, since there's a carry-over of 1 beyond the hundred millions place, that becomes the billions place. So 1..\n\nPutting all together:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred million), 3 (ten million),6 (million),0 (hundred thousand),1 (ten thousand),5 (thousand),0 (hundred),9 (ten),8 (one)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 79%|#######9  | 396/500 [20:01<05:18,  3.07s/it] 79%|#######9  | 397/500 [20:04<05:04,  2.96s/it]
