 , 4 6 6 , 6 9 8.\n\nWhich translates to:.\n\nHundred millions: 7.\n\nTen millions: 6.\n\nMillions: 9.\n\nHundred thousands: 4.\n\nTen thousands: 6.\n\nThousands: 6.\n\nHundreds: 6.\n\nTens: 9.\n\nOnes: 8.\n\nSimilarly, 944,438,224:.\n\n9 4 4 , 4 3 8 , 2 2 4.\n\nHundred millions: 9.\n\nTen millions: 4.\n\nMillions: 4.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:2.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo when adding, each corresponding digits:.\n\nStarting from ones place:.\n\nOnes: 8 + 4 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1..\n\nTens: 9 + 2 = 11, plus carryover 1 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1..\n\nHundreds:6 + 2 = 8, plus carryover 1 = 9. Write 9, no carryover..\n\nThousands place:6 (from first number) + 8 (from second number). Wait, thousands place in first number is 6 (since 769,466,698 is 769 million 466 thousand 698). Second number's thousands place is 8 (944,438,224 is 944 million 438 thousand 224). So 6 + 8 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1..\n\nTen thousands place:6 (first number) +3 (second number) = 9, plus carryover 1 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n\nHundred thousands place:4 (first) +4 (second) =8, plus carryover 1 =9. Write 9, no carryover..\n\nMillions place:9 (first) vs. 4 (second). Wait, wait, let's check positions again because maybe I messed up the places..\n\nWait, let me re-express the numbers with their digit positions clearly:.\n\nFirst number: 769,466,698.\n\nDigits (from left to right, starting with the highest place value):.\n\nHundred millions (100,000,000):7.\n\nTen millions (10,000,000):6.\n\nMillions (1,000,000):9.\n\nHundred thousands (100,000):4.\n\nTen thousands (10,000):6.\n\nThousands (1,000):6.\n\nHundreds (100):6.\n\nTens (10):9.\n\nOnes (1):8.\n\nSecond number:944,438,224.\n\nDigits:.\n\nHundred millions:9.\n\nTen millions:4.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:2.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo adding each corresponding digit:.\n\nStarting from ones (rightmost):.\n\n1. Ones place: 8 +4 =12 → digit 2, carryover 1..\n\n2. Tens place:9 +2=11 +1=12 → digit 2, carryover 1..\n\n3. Hundreds place:6 +2=8 +1=9 → digit 9, no carryover..\n\n4. Thousands place:6 +8=14 → digit 4, carryover 1..\n\n5. Ten thousands place:6 +3=9 +1=10 → digit 0, carryover1..\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:4 +4=8 +1=9 → digit9, no carryover..\n\n7. Millions place:9 +4=13 → digit3, carryover1..\n\n8. Ten millions place:6 +4=10 +1=11 → digit1, carryover1..\n\n9. Hundred millions place:7 +9=16 +1=17 → digit7, carryover1..\n\nWait, but after hundred millions place, there's a carryover 1, which would be the billions place. So the final sum would have a 1 in the billions place..\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nTens:2.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nHundred millions:7.\n\nAnd the carryover 1 becomes 1 in the billions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 80%|########  | 402/500 [20:19<05:36,  3.44s/it]
