ands), 0 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 7 (ones)..\n\nSecond number: 8 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (ones)..\n\nWait, that can't be right. Wait, 758,930,577 is 9 digits: 7-5-8-9-3-0-5-7-7. So positions are:.\n\n7 (100,000,000).\n5 (10,000,000).\n8 (1,000,000).\n9 (100,000).\n3 (10,000).\n0 (1,000).\n5 (100).\n7 (10).\n7 (1).\n\nSimilarly, 819,849,018 is:.\n\n8 (100,000,000).\n1 (10,000,000).\n9 (1,000,000).\n8 (100,000).\n4 (10,000).\n9 (1,000).\n0 (100).\n1 (10).\n8 (1).\n\nOkay, now adding each corresponding digit from right to left, with carry-over as needed..\n\nStarting with ones place: 7 + 8 = 15. Write 5, carry 1..\n\nTens place: 7 + 1 = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. Write 9, no carry..\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 0 = 5. Write 5, no carry..\n\nThousands place: 0 + 9 = 9. Write 9, no carry..\n\nTen thousands place: 3 + 4 = 7. Write 7, no carry..\n\nHundred thousands place: 9 + 8 = 17. Write 7, carry 1..\n\nMillions place: 8 (from first number) + 9 (from second) = 17, plus the carried 1 makes 18. Write 8, carry 1..\n\nTen millions place: 5 + 1 = 6, plus carried 1 makes 7. Write 7, no carry..\n\nHundred millions place: 7 + 8 = 15. Write 15 here since there's no higher place. So the digits would be 15 followed by the previous digits</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
