4.\n        --------------------.\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6 +4 =10 → 0, carry 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):3 +9=12 +1=13 →3, carry1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3 +0=3 +1=4 →4, carry0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3 +5=8 →8, carry0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7 +3=10 →0, carry1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3 +5=8 +1=9 →9, carry0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):2 +6=8 →8, carry0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3 +7=10 →0, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +5=13 +1=14 →4, carry1? Wait, but there are no more digits beyond hundred millions. So the total would be 1 (carryover) followed by 4, then the rest as per each position..\n\nWait, let me recheck each digit step by step carefully..\n\nLet me list each digit position for both numbers:.\n\nHundred millions place: 8 vs 5 → 8 +5 =13 (but any carryover from previous?).\n\nWait, actually, when adding, the leftmost digits are the highest place. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the order. Because when you write the numbers, the rightmost is ones, then tens, etc., so when aligned properly:.\n\nFirst number: 832,373,336.\n\nDigits (from right to left, positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1:6 (ones).\n\nPosition2:3 (tens).\n\nPosition3:3 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:2 (millions).\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:579,635,094.\n\nDigits (from right to left, positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition1:4.\n\nPosition2:9.\n\nPosition3:0.\n\nPosition4:5.\n\nPosition5:3.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition7:6.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition9:5.\n\nWait, wait, perhaps I need to check the positions again. Let's write the numbers with commas:.\n\n832,373,336 → 8 3 2 , 3 7 3 , 3 3 6.\n\nWhich breaks down as:.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:2.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nWait, maybe I'm mixing up the positions. Let me correct:.\n\nActually, the number 832,373,336 is structured as follows:.\n\n- 8 (hundred millions).\n- 3 (ten millions).\n- 2 (millions).\n- 3 (hundred thousands).\n- 7 (ten thousands).\n- 3 (thousands).\n- 3 (hundreds).\n- 3 (tens).\n- 6 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, 579,635,094 is:.\n\n- 5 (hundred millions).\n- 7 (ten millions).\n- 9 (millions).\n- 6 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 5 (thousands).\n- 0 (hundreds).\n- 9 (tens).\n- 4 (ones).\n\nWait, no. Wait, 579,635,094 is:.\n\nBreaking down: 579,635,094.\n\nHundred millions place:5.\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nMillions:9.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:9.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nHundred millions:8 (from first) +5 (from second) =13. But wait, we have to check if there's any carryover from the previous digit (ten millions place). Wait, no. Because the addition proceeds from right to left, so the carryover propagates to the next higher digit. Wait, no, when adding digits from right to left, the carryover affects the next digit to the left. So let's redo the addition properly, step by step..\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones place: 6 (from first number) +4 (second) =10. Write down 0, carryover 1..\n\n2. Tens place:3 (first) +9 (second) =12 +1 (carry) =13. Write down 3, carryover 1..\n\n3. Hundreds place:3 (first) +0 (second) =3 +1 (carry) =4. Write 4, no carry..\n\n4. Thousands place:3 (first) +5 (second) =8. Write 8, no carry..\n\n5. Ten thousands place:7 (first) +3 (second) =10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:3 (first) +5 (second) =8 +1 (carry) =9. Write 9, no carry..\n\n7. Millions place:2 (first) +6 (second) =8. Write 8, no carry..\n\n8. Ten millions place:3 (first) +7 (second) =10. Write 0, carryover 1..\n\n9. Hundred millions place:8 (first) +5 (second) =13 +1 (carry) =14. Write 4, carryover 1. But there's no higher place, so we need to add that carryover as a new digit on the left..\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nStarting from ones:.\n\nPosition1:0.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition3:4.\n\nPosition4:8.\n\nPosition5:0.\n\nPosition6:9.\n\nPosition7:8.\n\nPosition8:0.\n\nPosition9:4.\n\nWith a carryover of 1 at the end, so the final result is 1 followed by the digits from position9 to position1..\n\nSo the sum is 1,4 (from position9),0 (position8),8 (7),9 (6),0 (5),8 (4),4 (3),3 (2),0 (1). Wait, let me arrange them properly..\n\nWait, the digits are from highest place to lowest. So the carryover 1 is the highest digit, then the hundred millions place sum was 13 +1? Wait, maybe I got confused..\n\nWait, let's reconstruct the sum step by step:.\n\nAfter adding each digit, including carries:.\n\nStart from the right:.\n\nOnes:0 (carry 1).\n\nTens:3 (carry1).\n\nHundreds:4 (carry0).\n\nThousands:8 (carry0).\n\nTen thousands:0 (carry1).\n\nHundred thousands:9 (carry0).\n\nMillions:8 (carry0).\n\nTen millions:0 (carry1).\n\nHundred millions:8+5=13, plus carryover 1 from ten millions place? Wait, wait, maybe I messed up the order..\n\nWait, let's list each position again with their names and values:.\n\nPosition 1: Ones (6+4=10, sum digit 0, carry 1).\n\nPosition2: Tens (3+9=12 +1=13, sum 3, carry1).\n\nPosition3: Hundreds (3+0=3 +1=4, sum4, carry0).\n\nPosition4: Thousands (3+5=8, sum8, carry0).\n\nPosition5: Ten thousands (7+3=10, sum0, carry1).\n\nPosition6: Hundred thousands (3+5=8 +1=9, sum9, carry0).\n\nPosition7: Millions (2+6=8, sum8, carry0).\n\nPosition8: Ten millions (3+7=10, sum0, carry1).\n\nPosition9: Hundred millions (8+5=13 +1 (carry from ten millions)=14, sum4, carry1).\n\nThen, after processing all digits, there's a remaining carry of 1, which is added as a new digit at the beginning (billion's place)..\n\nSo the sum digits are:.\n\nFrom billion's place:1.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:0.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nWait, but when writing the number, it's 1 followed by 4 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 8 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),8 (thousands),4 (hundreds),3 (tens),0 (ones). Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me check again:.\n\nWait, the positions after adding:.\n\nAfter processing:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): sum is 4, carry1..\n\nBut we have to add that carry1 as a new digit. So the total digits would be:.\n\n1 (carryover) then the sum of hundred millions (4), ten millions (0), millions (8), hundred thousands (9), ten thousands (0), thousands (8), hundreds (4), tens (3), ones (0)..\n\nWait, but the sum of the hundreds millions place was 8 +5=13, plus the carryover from ten millions place, which was 1 (from ten millions addition). So 13 +1=14. So write 4, carryover 1. So yes, after that, the carryover 1 is added as a new digit</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
