bers again. Wait, the first number is 144,079,525. Let's break it down:.\n\nDigits of 144,079,525: 1 (hundred million), 4 (ten million), 4 (million), 0 (hundred thousand), 7 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 5 (hundred), 2 (ten), 5 (one)..\n\nSecond number 451,372,794: 4 (hundred million), 5 (ten million), 1 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 7 (ten thousand), 2 (thousand), 7 (hundred), 9 (ten), 4 (one)..\n\nWait, so when adding the millions place: first number has 4 (million place) and second number has 1 (million place). So 4 +1 =5. Plus any carryover? Let's check the previous step. The hundred-thousands place was 4+3+1=8, no carryover. So millions place: 4 +1 =5. So millions digit is 5, no carryover..\n\nTen-million place: first number has 4 (ten million), second has 5 (ten million). 4 +5 =9. No carryover from millions place. So ten-million digit is 9..\n\nHundred-million place: first number has 1, second has 4. 1 +4 =5. So hundred-million digit is 5</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
