dred thousands:8 vs 3.\nTen thousands:9 vs 6.\nThousands:8 vs 8.\nHundreds:0 vs 7.\nTens:9 vs 3.\nOnes:9 vs 9.\n\nWait, I think I messed up the places earlier. Let me redo the addition properly:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 9 +9 =18 → 8, carry 1..\n\nTens: 9 +3 =12 +1 =13 →3, carry 1..\n\nHundreds:0 +7 =7 +1=8 →8, no carry..\n\nThousands:8 +8=16 →6, carry1..\n\nTen thousands:9 +6=15 +1=16 →6, carry1..\n\nHundred thousands:8 +3=11 +1=12 →2, carry1..\n\nMillions place:4 +1=5 +1=6? Wait, wait. Let's see:.\n\nWait, the first number is 174,898,099. Let's break into digits:.\n\nDigits from left to right (starting with hundred millions):.\n\n1 (100,000,000), 7 (10,000,000), 4 (1,000,000), 8 (100,000),9 (10,000),8 (1,000),0 (100),9 (10),9 (1)..\n\nSecond number:481,368,739..\n\nDigits:.\n\n4 (100,000,000),8 (10,000,000),1 (1,000,000),3 (100,000),6 (10,000),8 (1,000),7 (100),3 (10),9 (1)..\n\nSo adding digit by digit:.\n\nHundred millions:1 +4 =5..\n\nTen millions:7 +8=15 →5, carry1..\n\nMillions:4 +1=5 +1 (carry) =6..\n\nHundred thousands:8 +3=11 →1, carry1..\n\nTen thousands:9 +6=15 +1=16 →6, carry1..\n\nThousands:8 +8=16 +1=17 →7, carry1..\n\nHundreds:0 +7=7 +1=8 →8, no carry..\n\nTens:9 +3=12 →2, carry1..\n\nWait, wait, no. Wait, earlier steps. Wait, I think I messed up the order when aligning the digits. Let me write both numbers one under the other:.\n\n       1 7 4 8 9 8 0 9 9.\n    + 4 8 1 3 6 8 7 3 9.\n    --------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 9 +9 =18 →8, carry1..\n\nPosition 2 (tens):9 +3=12 +1=13 →3, carry1..\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0 +7=7 +1=8 →8, no carry..\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8 +8=16 →6, carry1..\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9 +6=15 +1=16 →6, carry1..\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8 +3=11 +1=12 →2, carry1..\n\nPosition7 (millions):4 +1=5 +1 (carry from hundred thousands?) Wait, wait. Wait, the next position after hundred thousands is millions. So the first number has 4 in the millions place (since it's 174,898,099: 1 hundred million, 7 ten million, 4 million), and the second number has 1 in the millions place (since 481,368,739 is 4 hundred million,8 ten million,1 million). So millions place: 4 +1=5. Then, we had a carry of 1 from the hundred thousands place addition (8 +3=11, so carry1). Wait, but the hundred thousands place is position6, which we added 8 (from first number) and 3 (from second) to get 11, so we wrote 2 in hundred thousands and carry 1 to millions. So millions place: 4 +1=5 plus carry1=6. So millions place is 6, no carry..\n\nThen ten millions place:7 (from first number) and8 (from second number). 7 +8=15, so 5 in ten millions, carry1..\n\nHundred millions place:1 (from first) +4 (from second) +1 (carry)=6..\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from right:.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nTens:3.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nWait, but let me check:.\n\nWait, after adding each position:.\n\nPositions (from right to left):.\n\n1:8.\n\n2:3.\n\n3:8.\n\n4:6 (thousands place).\n\n5:6 (ten thousands).\n\n6:2 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:6 (millions).\n\n8:5 (ten millions).\n\n9:6 (hundred millions)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 85%|########4 | 423/500 [22:11<04:20,  3.39s/it]
