sands:3 +7=10 → write 0, carry 1..\n\nMillions place:8 +3=11 +1=12 → write 2, carry 1..\n\nTen-millions:9 +5=14 +1=15 → write 5, carry 1..\n\nHundred-millions:1 +9=10 +1=11 → write 1, carry 1..\n\nBut wait, the hundred-millions place: the first number has 1 (from 198 million) and the second has 9 (from 957 million). So adding those: 1+9=10, plus the carryover 1 from the previous step. Wait, wait, when we add the ten-millions place, we carried over 1. So the hundred-millions place is 1 (from first number) +9 (from second) +1 (carry) =11. So write 1 and carry over 1. But since there are no higher digits left, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning. So the final sum would be 1,1..., etc..\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from ones:.\n\n4, then 4,5,3,7,0,2,5,1, and the final carryover 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
