messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 893028019 and 990584283?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 893,028,019 and 990,584,283. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step. Let me start by writing them down one under the other, making sure the digits line up properly by place value..\n\nLet's see:.\n\n  893,028,019.\n+990,584,283.\n--------------.\n .\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The ones digits are 9 and 3. Adding those together: 9 + 3 = 12. I write down 2 in the ones place and carry over 1 to the tens place..\n\nNext is the tens place. The digits are 1 and 8. Wait, wait, wait. Let me check again. The original numbers are 893,028,019 and 990,584,283. Let me break them down digit by digit to make sure I'm not mixing up any places..\n\nBreaking down 893,028,019:.\n- 9 (ones).\n- 1 (tens).\n- 0 (hundreds).\n- 8 (thousands).\n- 2 (ten thousands).\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 0 (millions).\n- 3 (ten millions).\n- 9 (hundred millions).\n- 8 (actually, wait, maybe I miscounted. Let me count the digits properly. Let's write it out:.\n\n893,028,019 is:.\n9 (ones).\n1 (tens).\n0 (hundreds).\n8 (thousands).\n2 (ten thousands).\n0 (hundred thousands).\n3 (millions).\n0 (ten millions).\n9 (hundred millions).\nWait, no. Wait, the number is 893,028,019. Breaking it down from right to left:.\n\nPositions (from right): ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands, millions, ten millions, hundred millions..\n\nSo 893,028,019:.\n\nDigits (right to left):.\n1. Ones: 9.\n2. Tens: 1.\n3. Hundreds: 0.\n4. Thousands: 8.\n5. Ten thousands: 2.\n6. Hundred thousands: 0.\n7. Millions: 3.\n8. Ten millions: 9.\n9. Hundred millions: 8.\n\nWait, no. Wait, the number is 893,028,019. Let's separate the digits:.\n\nFrom left to right: 8 9 3 0 2 8 0 1 9..\n\nBut when adding, we start from the right. So the rightmost digit is 9 (ones), then moving left: 1 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 0 (ten millions?), Wait, maybe I need to parse this correctly..\n\nWait, 893,028,019 is written as 893 million, 028 thousand, and 019. So:.\n\n- Hundred millions place: 8.\n- Ten millions: 9.\n- Millions: 3.\n- Hundred thousands: 0.\n- Ten thousands: 2.\n- Thousands: 8.\n- Hundreds: 0.\n- Tens: 1.\n- Ones: 9.\n\nSimilarly, 990,584,283:.\n\n- Hundred millions: 9.\n- Ten millions: 9.\n- Millions: 0.\n- Hundred thousands: 5.\n- Ten thousands: 8.\n- Thousands: 4.\n- Hundreds: 2.\n- Tens: 8.\n- Ones: 3.\n\nSo when aligned:.\n\n          8 9 3 0 2 8 0 1 9.\n        +9 9 0 5 8 4 2 8 3.\n        -------------------.\n\nLet me write each digit position by place:.\n\nStarting from ones (rightmost):.\n\nOnes: 9 + 3 = 12 → write 2, carry 1..\n\nTens: 1 (from first number) + 8 (from second) = 9 + carry 1 = 10 → write 0, carry 1..\n\nHundreds: 0 (from first) + 2 (from second) = 2 + carry 1 = 3 → write 3, no carry..\n\nThousands: 8 (first) +4 (second) = 12 → write 2, carry 1..\n\nTen thousands: 2 (first) +8 (second) =10 + carry 1 =11 → write 1, carry 1..\n\nHundred thousands:0 (first) +5 (second) =5 + carry 1 =6 → write 6, no carry..\n\nMillions:3 (first) +0 (second) =3 + no carry =3 → write 3..\n\nTen millions:9 (first) +9 (second) =18 → write 8, carry 1..\n\nHundred millions:8 (first) +9 (second) =17 + carry 1 (from previous) =18 → write 8, carry 1..\n\nWait, but now we have an extra carry-over at the hundred millions place. Since we're dealing with 9-digit numbers, adding the hundred millions digits (8 +9) gives 17 plus the carry 1 makes 18. So we write 8 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the next higher place, which is billions. So the final sum would have a 1 in the billions place..\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the ones:.\n\nOnes: 2.\n\nTens:0.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nBillions:1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 87%|########6 | 434/500 [22:40<03:00,  2.74s/it] 87%|########7 | 435/500 [22:42<02:54,  2.69s/it]
