mbers with their digits separated by commas for clarity:.\n\n214,759,092: Digits from right to left (ones to hundred millions):.\nOnes: 2.\nTens:9.\nHundreds:0.\nThousands:9.\nTen thousands:5.\nHundred thousands:7.\nMillions:4.\nTen millions:1.\nHundred millions:2.\n\nSimilarly, 861,630,836:.\nOnes:6.\nTens:3.\nHundreds:8.\nThousands:0.\nTen thousands:3.\nHundred thousands:6.\nMillions:1.\nTen millions:6.\nHundred millions:8.\n\nNow adding each place:.\n\nOnes: 2 +6 =8. So ones digit 8..\n\nTens:9 +3=12. Write 2, carry 1..\n\nHundreds:0 +8=8 +1 (carry) =9. No carry..\n\nThousands:9 +0=9. No carry..\n\nTen thousands:5 +3=8. No carry..\n\nHundred thousands:7 +6=13. Write 3, carry 1..\n\nMillions place:4 (from first number) +1 (from second number) =5, plus the carried 1 makes 6. So millions digit is 6, no carry..\n\nTen millions place:1 (from first) +6 (from second) =7. So 7 here, no carry..\n\nHundred millions place:2 +8=10. So write 0 and carry 1. But since there are no higher places, we add that carry as a new digit to the left..\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right: 8 (ones), 2 (tens), 9 (hundreds),9 (thousands),8 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),6 (millions),7 (ten millions),0 (hundred millions), and the carried 1 becomes the next digit: 1 (billions place?)..\n\nWait, but the original numbers are 9-digit numbers (hundred millions place is the highest). So adding them could result in a 10-digit number. Let me confirm:.\n\n214,759,092 + 861,630,836..\n\nLet me do a rough estimate: 200 million + 800 million = 1 billion. 14 million + 61 million = 75 million. So total around 1,075,000,000. But precise addition:.\n\nAdding step by step:.\n\n     214,759,092.\n   + 861,630,836</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
