46,160.\nDigits (from right to left):.\n0 (ones).\n6 (tens).\n1 (hundreds).\n6 (thousands).\n4 (ten thousands).\n7 (hundred thousands).\n4 (millions).\n3 (ten millions).\n7 (hundred millions).\n\nNow, adding each corresponding digit starting from ones:.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 0 = 4. Sum digit:4. Carryover:0..\n\nTens place:6 +6=12. Sum digit:2. Carryover:1..\n\nHundreds place:5 +1 +1 (carry)=7. Sum digit:7. Carryover:0..\n\nThousands place:1 +6=7. Sum digit:7. Carryover:0..\n\nTen-thousands place:3 +4=7. Sum digit:7. Carryover:0..\n\nHundred-thousands place:6 +7=13. Sum digit:3. Carryover:1..\n\nMillions place:5 +4=9 +1 (carry)=10. Sum digit:0. Carryover:1..\n\nTen-millions place:4 +3=7 +1=8. Sum digit:8. Carryover:0..\n\nHundred-millions place:3 +7=10. Sum digit:0. Carryover:1..\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place sum is 3 +7=10. But since these are the highest digits, we need to include the carryover. So sum here is 10, which would be 0 with a carryover of 1. But since there are no more digits left, that carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
