ousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 9 (ones)..\n\nSo when adding, let's align each place:.\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\n0 (ones) +9 (ones) =9.\n\nTens:5 +1=6.\n\nHundreds:4 +3=7.\n\nThousands:6 +4=10 → write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen thousands:6 (from first number) +2 (from second number) + carryover 1=9.\n\nHundred thousands:0 (from first number) +8 (from second number) =8.\n\nMillions:5 (from first) +8 (from second)=13. Write 3, carry 1..\n\nTen millions:1 (from first) +1 (from second) +1 (carry)=3..\n\nHundred millions:8 (from first) +5 (from second)=13. So write 3, carry 1..\n\nThen, since there are no higher digits, the carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the billions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
