ber's next digit is 4. Wait, wait. Wait, let me check the numbers again. Wait, 783,944,466 is a 9-digit number: 7 8 3 9 4 4 4 6 6. And 473,642,303 is 4 7 3 6 4 2 3 0 3. So when aligned:.\n\n          7 8 3 9 4 4 4 6 6.\n        +4 7 3 6 4 2 3 0 3.\n        ---------------------.\n\nSo starting from the right (ones place):.\n\n6 + 3 = 9 (ones).\n6 + 0 = 6 (tens).\n4 + 3 = 7 (hundreds).\n4 + 2 = 6 (thousands).\n9 + 4 = 13 (ten thousands: write 3, carryover 1).\n3 + 6 = 9 plus carryover 1 makes 10 (hundred thousands: write 0, carryover 1).\n8 + 3 = 11 plus carryover 1 makes 12 (millions: write 2, carryover 1).\n7 + 7 = 14 plus carryover 1 makes 15 (ten millions: write 5, carryover 1).\nFinally, the hundred millions place: the first number has 7, the second has 4. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 783,944,466. Breaking it down:.\n\nDigits (from right to left):.\n\nPosition 1: 6 (ones).\nPosition 2: 6 (tens).\nPosition 3: 4 (hundreds).\nPosition 4: 4 (thousands).\nPosition 5: 4 (ten thousands).\nPosition 6: 9 (hundred thousands).\nPosition 7: 3 (millions).\nPosition 8: 8 (ten millions).\nPosition 9: 7 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 473,642,303.\n\nDigits:.\n\nPosition 1:3 (ones).\nPosition 2:0 (tens).\nPosition 3:3 (hundreds).\nPosition 4:2 (thousands).\nPosition 5:4 (ten thousands).\nPosition 6:6 (hundred thousands).\nPosition 7:3 (millions).\nPosition 8:7 (ten millions).\nPosition 9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, so when adding, each position:.\n\nPositions 1 to 9:.\n\n1:6+3=9.\n2:6+0=6.\n3:4+3=7.\n4:4+2=6.\n5:4+4=8.\n6:9+6=15 (so 5, carryover 1).\n7:3+3=6 +1 (carryover) =7.\n8:8+7=15 (5, carryover 1).\n9:7+4=11 +1 (carryover) =12. So here, the hundred millions place would be 2, carryover 1, but since there are no higher digits, that carryover becomes a new digit at the billions place: 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
