reaking it down: 5 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 4 (ones)..\n\nSecond number: 651,658,122. Breaking down: 6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 2 (ones)..\n\nWait, earlier when I did the ten-thousands place, the first number has 8 (ten thousands), the second has 5 (ten thousands). So 8 +5 =13, right. So that's correct, carry over 1. Then the hundred thousands place: first number is 2, second is 6. 2 +6=8, plus the carryover 1 makes 9. So that's correct. Then millions place: first number is 4, second is 1. 4+1=5, no carryover..\n\nNext, ten millions place: first number has 3, second has 5. 3 +5=8. So that's 8 in the ten millions place..\n\nHundred millions place: first number is 5, second is 6. 5+6=11. So here, we write 1 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the next higher place, which is billions..\n\nSince these numbers are both 9-digit, adding them might result in a 10-digit number. Let me check each digit again:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 4 +2=6..\n\nTens: 0 +2=2..\n\nHundreds:1 +1=2..\n\nThousands:0 +8=8..\n\nTen thousands:8 +5=13 (write 3, carry 1)..\n\nHundred thousands:2 +6 +1=9..\n\nMillions:4 +1=5..\n\nTen millions:3 +5=8..\n\nHundred millions:5 +6=11 (write 1, carry 1)..\n\nThen, since there's a carryover 1 in the hundred millions place, that would go to the billions place, which is the next higher. So the final sum would be 1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, let me assemble that..\n\nWait, when adding, after the hundred millions place, the carryover 1 becomes the billions digit. So the sum should be 1,1 (billion and hundred million), then 8 (ten million), 5 (million), 9 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand)